addRun("a-second-judge-and-a-frontier-writer--writer-claude-haiku-5-5--harvey-labs--claude-code--corporate-governance-draft-compliance-regulatory", {"meta": {"benchmark": "harvey-labs", "harness": "claude-code", "model": "qwen3.8-27b", "task_title": "Draft Code of Business Conduct and Ethics for Newly Public Biopharmaceutical Company", "axis": "minute", "set": "a-second-judge-and-a-frontier-writer", "arm": "writer-claude-haiku-5-5"}, "events": [{"id": "event_001", "status": "unasked"}, {"id": "event_002", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_003", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_005", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_004", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_006", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_007", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_008", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_009", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_010", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_011", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_012", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_013", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_014", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_015", "status": "unasked"}, {"id": "event_016", "status": "unasked"}, {"id": "event_017", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_018", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_019", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_020", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_021", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_022", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_023", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_024", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_025", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_026", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_027", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_028", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_029", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_030", "status": "unasked"}], "questions": [{"id": "question_01", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks what the execution plan in /app/workspace/process/notes.md fixed (two deliverables, a reading order with the Calloway email first because it is the drafting instructions and the Thornberry assessment next because it likely holds the \"flagged gaps,\" and a markdown-draft / pandoc-build / round-trip-verify workflow) and why those two sources topped the order. Per card_0004 and card_0005, the notes contained exactly that plan. The report only gestures at this step: its sequence table says the agent \"wrote process notes (card 5)\" with no content, and its \"Source reading\" row lists what was actually read (\"Read the instruction email (6), the Thornberry assessment (8), the draft board resolution (10)...\") without attributing that order to the up-front plan or giving any reason for it. Elsewhere the report describes the final two deliverables and the pandoc build, but as outcomes of later work, not as commitments fixed in notes.md, and \"round-trip\" verification appears nowhere. Since the report never states what the plan fixed or why Calloway and Thornberry were placed first, the answer is absent (only vague topic mentions).", "evidence": ["card_0004", "card_0005"]}, {"id": "question_02", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The report-only reader returned NOT_IN_REPORT, but its own \"unlocatable_evidence\" text is in fact the report's section 2 verbatim, so that is a reader miss, not a report miss. The report answers the question: section 2 identifies the trigger document as James Calloway's (General Counsel and Corporate Secretary) drafting-instructions email dated January 27, 2025 (card 6), matching card_0006; it states the deliverables (a single Code plus a companion drafting memorandum) and the timeline (draft by February 28, board pre-meeting March 10, adoption March 18, 2025), matching the gold answer; and it gives the regulatory scope (Nasdaq Listing Rule 5610, SOX 406 / Item 406 of Reg S-K, plus a local-law savings clause). Section 3's sequence table places the setup work (cards 1-5, \"Inventory and notes\") before \"Source reading (cards 6-21): Read the instruction email (6), the Thornberry assessment (8)...\", which captures the transition from task setup into systematic ingestion of the sources that card_0007 shows the agent undertaking. All quoted passages were verified as exact contiguous substrings of input/sample_report.html.", "evidence": ["card_0006", "card_0007"]}, {"id": "question_03", "answer": "Before drafting, the agent recorded the discrepancies in a fact inventory it wrote as process/source-facts.md (card 22), covering \"people, figures, the 14 findings, and source discrepancies,\" and the companion memorandum received a \"source-discrepancy list\" with its resolutions. Reconciliations were made by adopting the value supported by the instruction email / charter / resolution and using it in the new drafts — e.g., the new drafts use 235 Binney Street (per the Charter, draft resolution, and email) rather than 225 Binney Street from the legacy policies, with the legacy corrections carried as an outstanding item in the memorandum — and by correcting individual facts in the memo (Brenner's title fixed to Clinical Research Associate, card 49). Some recorded discrepancies were never corrected, e.g., the misattributed \"Section 5.3,\" which the fact inventory repeats with \"nothing in the record corrects it.\"", "quote": "3. Fact inventory 22 Wrote process/source-facts.md , covering people, figures, the 14 findings, and source discrepancies. The note contains errors (section 6).", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The question asks how the agent reconciled the disagreeing source facts and where it recorded the resolutions. The report answers the \"where\" directly: step 3 (card 22), before drafting, wrote process/source-facts.md covering \"people, figures, the 14 findings, and source discrepancies,\" and the memorandum carries a \"source-discrepancy list\" plus an outstanding-items table. It answers the \"how\" with concrete, trace-grounded resolutions: the new drafts adopt 235 Binney Street over the legacy 225 references, with the legacy corrections carried as an outstanding memo item; Brenner's title is corrected from \"CRO\" to Clinical Research Associate; and the facilitation-exception cite is identified as actually sitting in the policy's §4 (the \"Section 5.3\" wording being a misattribution). This matches card_0021 (read all ten sources, then record facts and discrepancies before drafting) and card_0022 (source-facts.md with a \"Source discrepancies found (for memo § 'drafting judgments')\" section whose resolutions — use 235, use autoimmune, cite §4, treat the supplemental update as part of the report — set the winning values). The report does not spell out the therapy-area (genetic vs autoimmune) or headcount (340 vs 410) resolutions, but it does communicate the material mechanism and location with multiple correct examples, so the report-only reader's extraction is faithful and the report communicates the material trace-grounded answer.", "evidence": ["card_0021", "card_0022"]}, {"id": "question_04", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The report explains the choice: the deliverables table states the Code contains \"Section 12, the 'Supplemental Code of Ethics for Senior Financial Officers,' which matches the draft resolution's designation,\" tying the numbering directly to the resolution (card_0010 shows the resolution read at turn 15; card_0012 shows the agent flagging Section 12 as \"a key structural constraint\"). The sequence table notes the agent \"Drafted the Code in markdown ... 15 numbered sections plus an appendix,\" and the verification table records the check that the \"Section 12 title matches the draft resolution ... Matches.\" (card 59), with the discussion section quoting the memo's \"no changes currently anticipated — the draft aligns\" (card 26; consistent with card_0023's drafting intent and card_0024's written memo). Together these communicate that Section 12 was the SFO Code because the draft board resolution designated Section 12 that way, and the topic-organized 15-section layout was built to align with the resolution so it needed no edits. The report-only reader returned NOT_IN_REPORT and listed this passage as unlocatable, but it is present verbatim (line 133); that extraction miss does not change the score.", "evidence": ["card_0010", "card_0012", "card_0023", "card_0024", "card_0026"]}, {"id": "question_05", "answer": "The report does not give an explicit, policy-by-policy \"superseded vs. retained\" list for the draft. What it shows is: in the inventory phase the agent read six policy documents (cards 13, 14, 16, 17, 19, 20). Of Greenfield's existing standalone documents, only the 2021 handbook is described as superseded by the draft — \"Superseded under Code §1.3.\" The rest appear to have been retained: the Whistleblower and Social Media policies remain in place pending \"conforming amendments\" (a listed outstanding item), and a \"standalone Data Privacy Policy\" is still expected \"by about April 2025.\" The outdated 2019 conflict-of-interest policy's rules and the 2022 facilitation-payments exception were folded into Code §4 and §6.2 respectively, and the $5,000 internal disclosure threshold was kept.", "quote": "5 High Conflict-of-interest policy outdated (2019) Conflicts rules in Code §4 Initial related-party inventory and D&O questionnaires on adoption 6 High Facilitation-payments exception in the 2022 policy Prohibited in Code §6.2, with a narrow duress exception only Memo cross-reference misattributed (section 6) 7 High No data privacy program Principles in Code §10 Standalone Data Privacy Policy by about April 2025 8 High No related-party procedures Code §5: Audit Committee pre-approval at $120,000 or more; recusal; inventory Audit Committee review of the Sørensen consulting arrangement; ratification of the Solstice contract with Castellano and Chang recused 9 High 2021 handbook has no public-company obligations Superseded under Code §1.3 Conforming amendments to the Whistleblower and Social Media policies 10 High Anti-corruption training never conducted Training provisions in Code §2.5 Training program, $120,000 11 Medium $5,000 conflict-of-interest threshold misaligned $5,000 internal disclosure kept; $120,000 and above escalates None listed separately 12 Medium Whistleblower policy lacks regulatory specificity Code §13.4 (SOX 806, Dodd-Frank 21F, SEC program) Whistleblower Policy amendments", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks how the draft handled Greenfield's existing standalone policies: which were superseded and which retained. Per card_0024, the draft Code's §1.3 supersedes three items (the Employee Handbook's \"Ethics\" section of August 16, 2021, the Conflict of Interest Policy of March 12, 2019, and the Anti-Corruption Compliance Policy of April 3, 2022), retains three as supplemental (Insider Trading, Whistleblower, and Social Media Policies), and provides that the Code controls in any conflict; card_0026's memo likewise refers to the draft's \"supersession list.\" The report never gives this superseded-versus-retained list. Its only substantive mention is one cell in the Thornberry findings matrix — \"Superseded under Code §1.3\" for the 2021 handbook — which identifies a single superseded item and still does not state the superseded list, the retained list, or the Code-controls rule. Adjacent rows note that conflicts rules went into Code §4 and the 2022 facilitation-payments exception into §6.2, and that Whistleblower/Social Media/Insider Trading amendments are outstanding items, but these are finding-to-section mappings and open items, not a statement of which existing policies the draft displaces versus keeps in force. The report-only reader likewise noted the report does not give an explicit policy-by-policy list. This is a vague topic mention that does not materially answer the question, so it is missing_from_report.", "evidence": ["card_0024", "card_0026"]}, {"id": "question_06", "answer": "The second deliverable was a privileged drafting memorandum (drafting-memorandum.docx, about 4,700 words), written as from a named partner at Whitfield & Crane LLP to the company's General Counsel. It covered Sections I to IX: a finding-by-finding matrix for the 14 Thornberry items, resolutions of the client's specific questions, a source-discrepancy list, and an outstanding-items table of 15 rows.", "quote": "The agent produced two Word documents in /app/workspace/ : a Code of Business Conduct and Ethics (about 8,400 words) and a privileged drafting memorandum (about 4,700 words). The memorandum is written as from a named partner at Whitfield & Crane LLP to the company's General Counsel.", "read": "complete", "reads": 2, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The report directly answers the question: after the Code, the second deliverable was a privileged drafting memorandum, written from a partner at Whitfield & Crane LLP (Sarah Lindström, named in section 2) to the company's General Counsel (James Calloway, also identified as GC in section 2), drafted at /tmp/draft/memo.md (card 26). Its coverage description — privileged marking, Sections I to IX, a finding-by-finding matrix for the 14 Thornberry findings, resolutions of the client's specific questions, a source-discrepancy list, and an outstanding-items table of 15 rows — matches what card_0026 shows the memorandum contains (source-document list, structural decisions, source-policy resolutions, responses to all fourteen Thornberry findings, and a 15-item outstanding action list with owners and timing). The report does not separately enumerate the memo's Section I source-document table or its structural-decisions section, but it communicates the material trace-grounded answer with no contradiction. The report-only reader's first attempt returned NOT_IN_REPORT; its second attempt located the passages, and the report does in fact answer the question, so the report is scored on its text.", "evidence": ["card_0025", "card_0026"]}, {"id": "question_07", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The report gestures at the topic without answering the question. In section 6.2 it notes only that the draft board resolution \"lets the Board, or the Audit Committee \\\"if delegated,\\\" approve waivers\" and that \"The Charter gives the Audit Committee authority over executive-officer and director waivers (§V.D.2) and bars subcommittee delegation of that authority (§VI),\" and it elsewhere mentions the client's waiver request (four-business-day Form 8-K Item 5.05 disclosure), a table row that the agent edited \"the waiver question\" in the memorandum, and a next-steps item to \"confirm whether a Board delegation to the Audit Committee is needed.\" But it never states what the memorandum actually answered: that the agent recommended waiver authority sit with the Audit Committee as delegated by the Board, in the formulation \"Board or, as delegated by the Board, the Audit Committee,\" because the Charter (§V.D.2) already confers the authority so no Charter amendment is needed, the Adoption Resolution already tracks the formulation, Charter §VI makes it non-delegable to subcommittees, and the committee's compliance-oversight mandate makes it the natural home — and it does not mention the explicit rejection of a Nominating & Governance co-role or the memorandum's §14.3 Item 5.05 disclosure language. That is the trace-grounded answer in card_0026 (§V.C), and it is absent; the report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT is correct.", "evidence": ["card_0026"]}, {"id": "question_08", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The report answers the question directly. Its section 6.2 enumerates all 15 outstanding items from the memorandum, matching the table in card_0026 (placeholder reception number removal, 24/7 hotline vendor with the $85,000 budget, Brenner remedial review before Code rollout, Sørensen and Solstice Audit Committee actions, related-party inventory and D&O questionnaires, Insider Trading Policy amendment, training program, Data Privacy Policy, CCO search, Code distribution and acknowledgments, website posting and Exhibit 14 filing, CEO letter, conforming amendments and address corrections, Swiss-law supplement for Basel). The summary and next-steps sections reinforce that these were deliberately left open for the client and none were completed. The report-only reader returned NOT_IN_REPORT, but its own unlocatable-evidence field quotes the exact passage that answers the question; that is an extraction miss, not a report gap. The one minor deviation — \"Cambridge reception number\" instead of the placeholder number 617-555-0142 — does not materially misidentify the item, so the report carries the trace-grounded answer.", "evidence": ["card_0026"]}, {"id": "question_09", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "unsupported_or_contradicted", "score": 0, "rationale": "The gold answer (cards 27–28) is a specific consistency fix made immediately before the .docx build: the YAML cover line of /tmp/draft/code.md was changed from \"Adopted by the Board of Directors | Effective March 18, 2025\" to \"For adoption by the Board of Directors on March 18, 2025 | Effective March 18, 2025\", because the Feb 28, 2025 pre-adoption draft cannot state the Code was already adopted. The report addresses this edit only in a single sequence-table cell, which misdescribes it: card_0028 shows the effective date was already \"March 18, 2025\", so nothing was \"changed … to adoption on March 18, 2025\"; the actual change was the adoption-status wording (\"Adopted\" → \"For adoption … on March 18, 2025\"). The report never gives the reason at all — no mention of the cover/front-matter line, the pre-adoption timing, or why the edit was made — so the \"why\" half of the question is unaddressed, and the wording of the change is not carried by the report's own evidence. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT reflects the same gap; the one-line table cell is at best a vague, imprecise gesture at the topic, not a materially correct answer.", "evidence": ["card_0027", "card_0028"]}, {"id": "question_10", "answer": "The chunk-ending build was the agent's first pandoc build, which built both .docx deliverables (the Code and the memorandum). It failed in that the build chain exited with code 127 because a command called `file`, which is not installed on the system, was invoked; despite the failure, both files were written, and the agent did not rerun that step (its later zip check passed for both files).", "quote": "Failed build step. The first pandoc build exited with code 127 because a command called file , which is not installed (card 29). Both .docx files were written. The agent did not rerun that step; its zip check (card 31) passed for both.", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The report answers both halves of the question. What the chunk-ending build did: it built both .docx deliverables with pandoc (phase \"5. First build and checks,\" cards 29-43), and the artifact table confirms the output (the memorandum at \"25,390 bytes at first build (card 29),\" matching card_0029's ls output). What failed in it: the build chain exited with code 127 because the trailing `file` command is not installed, so the files were only written, not verified by that step — verification came later via the zip check (card 31). This matches card_0029 exactly (pandoc for code.md and memo.md, ls -la listing both files at 33,730 and 25,390 bytes, exit code 127 from `file`). The report neither overstates the success nor omits the failure, so the reader's answer is well supported by the report.", "evidence": ["card_0029"]}, {"id": "question_11", "answer": "After building the two .docx deliverables, the agent began verification by checking the zip structure of both files (card 31). The first, structural check passed for both files: each contained the core parts and a non-trivial document body.", "quote": "Zip and OOXML structure of both .docx files 31 Passed for both files. Each contains the core parts and a non-trivial document body.", "read": "complete", "reads": 2, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The report answers both halves of the question. Section 3 (group 5, cards 29–43) places the zip structure check on card 31 immediately after both .docx files were built, and the verification table in Section 7 states the first check — \"Zip and OOXML structure of both .docx files\" (card 31) — \"Passed for both files. Each contains the core parts and a non-trivial document body.\" That matches card_0030/card_0031: the agent's first verification step after creating the files was a python3 zipfile structural check of both deliverables, and both were confirmed valid OOXML packages with the expected internal parts and non-trivial document bodies. The report omits finer details (16 parts each, the 119,378/92,175-byte document.xml sizes, and the exact transition phrase \"Now let me verify they're genuine OOXML documents\"), but the material substance — a structural OOXML validity check run first and passed for both files — is clearly communicated. Note: the report-only reader's first attempt returned NOT_IN_REPORT, a miss; the report does contain the answer, as the reader's second located attempt and my own search confirm.", "evidence": ["card_0030", "card_0031"]}, {"id": "question_12", "answer": "The agent verified the content of the two .docx deliverables by converting them to plain text and then running string/occurrence-count checks for required terms on that text (it also checked the zip/OOXML structure, but did not view the files in a rendered form). The conversion produced plain-text versions of both documents: about 8,400 words for the Code and about 4,700 words for the memorandum. The first term checks run on that text returned zeros for several required terms (including \"National Labor Relations\"), so the agent reran them on flattened text, and its \"Chang\" hits were false positives for \"changes\".", "quote": "Checked the zip structure (card 31) and converted both files to plain text (card 33). The first term checks returned zeros for several required terms (card 34). The agent reran them on flattened text (card 36)", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The question asks how the agent chose to verify the .docx content and what the conversion produced. Card_0032 shows the decision to round-trip the .docx files back through pandoc to plain text and check every required element, and card_0033 shows that conversion producing code_check.txt and memo_check.txt at 8,396 and 4,653 words (13,049 total). The report communicates this substance: in its group table it says the agent \"converted both files to plain text (card 33)\" and then ran term checks, rerunning them on flattened text (card 36), and in the verification section it states the plain-text conversion of both files was done, reporting \"about 8,400 words (Code) and about 4,700 words (memo).\" The report rounds the exact word counts (8,396 -> \"about 8,400\", 4,653 -> \"about 4,700\") and does not state the agent's \"as the evaluator will\" rationale or the 13,049 total, but the rounded figures are explicitly hedged with \"about\" and the material decision and outcome are faithfully conveyed. The report-only reader's extraction is consistent with the report.", "evidence": ["card_0032", "card_0033"]}, {"id": "question_13", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "Card_0036 shows the agent flattened code_check.txt to code_flat.txt and found five of the previously zero-count strings occur exactly once (line-wrap artifacts: \"Item 406 of Regulation S-K\", \"full, fair, accurate, timely, and understandable\", \"General Data Protection Regulation\", \"National Labor Relations\", \"Swiss Federal Act on Data Protection\"), while \"pre-approval\" (0) and \"GDPR\" (0) remained genuinely absent. The report never reports this result. Its only mentions are the generic: \"The agent reran them on flattened text (card 36)\" in the sequence table and \"The agent reran on flattened text (card 36)\" in the verification table (with \"National Labor Relations\" listed among the zero counts from the first pass, card 34). The report does not say the zeros were extraction/line-wrap artifacts, does not list any recovered count of 1, and does not identify \"pre-approval\" or \"GDPR\" as the truly absent forms. That is a vague topic mention that does not materially answer the question, so it is missing_from_report. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT extraction agrees with the report text.", "evidence": ["card_0036"]}, {"id": "question_14", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The question asks why the agent declared the Code clean despite non-zero hits for \"Chang\" and \"Calloway\". The report's verification section answers it with the trace-grounded reason: the \"Chang\" hits were false positives for the ordinary word \"changes\" (matching card_0038's context greps showing \"ending any changes to the Board\", \"circumstances change\", etc.), and the private-matter strings did not appear in the Code (matching card_0037, where every other private string had 0 occurrences), which is why the agent declared the Code clean and moved on (card_0039). One detail of the gold answer is absent: the report never states that the \"Calloway\" hits were the intended, public name \"James Calloway, General Counsel\" (the Code's addressee), and it says nothing about the name-pattern check showing only \"Annika Sørensen\" and \"Rachel Dominguez\". That is a gap, but the material explanation — the non-zero hits were benign ordinary words and no private-matter string actually appeared — is communicated, and everything the report says is consistent with cards 37–39. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT verdict is a miss; the report does address the question.", "evidence": ["card_0037", "card_0038", "card_0039"]}, {"id": "question_15", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks what the first required-facts scan of the drafting memorandum returned (grep -c per required string on memo_flat.txt: 0 for \"Wei-Lin Chang\", \"Section 806\", \"whistleblower hotline\", \"Annika Sørensen\", \"Marcus Whitfield\", \"Elena Volkova\", \"Lars\", \"Nasdaq Global Select\", \"October 11, 2024\", and all six source-policy dates, 1 for most other facts) and why that result was inconclusive (memo_flat.txt was a single flattened line, so grep -c counts lines and is all-or-nothing — a zero could mean missing or merely rephrased). The report never communicates either part for the memo scan. It only gestures at the topic: in section 3 it says \"The first term checks returned zeros for several required terms (card 34)\" and in section 7 it lists \"Required terms present (occurrence counts)\" for \"34, 36, 38, 40, 42, 43, 56, 58\" with the result \"First pass returned zeros for several terms, including \\\"National Labor Relations\\\" (card 34). The agent reran on flattened text (card 36).\" Both passages attribute the zeros to the Code checks (cards 34/36), not the card_0040 memo scan; they do not identify which memo facts scored zero, do not contrast them with the facts that scored one, and give no reason the memo result was inconclusive (no mention of grep -c line counting on a single-line file). The reader's NOT_IN_REPORT extraction is therefore correct, and the material trace-grounded answer is absent from the report.", "evidence": ["card_0040"]}, {"id": "question_16", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks why the agent re-checked the memo's zero-count facts with a different method (the flattened file made grep -c line-based/all-or-nothing) and what that re-check established (e.g., \"806\" 2 hits, \"Chang\" 7 hits always without a first name, \"Sørensen\" 8 surname-only hits, \"Nasdaq\" 1 hit only as \"Nasdaq 5610\", \"Whitfield\" 4 hits all the law firm, and Volkova/Lars/full \"September\"/\"October 11\"/source dates genuinely 0). I searched the report directly (grep for zero/grep/re-check/occurrence/flattened/Volkova/806/Marcus/October 11/line-based/etc.): none of that is there. The report only gestures at the surrounding topic: \"The first term checks returned zeros for several required terms (card 34). The agent reran them on flattened text (card 36) and reported that private matters were absent from the Code (card 37).\" and \"First pass returned zeros for several terms, including \"National Labor Relations\" (card 34). The agent reran on flattened text (card 36). Its \"Chang\" hits were false positives for \"changes\" (card 38).\" Both passages describe the earlier Code-check sequence (cards 34-38), not the memo zero-fact re-check in cards 41-43: no reason tied to line-based grep -c, no occurrence-count method, and none of the per-term counts or context disambiguations that the re-check established. This is a vague topic mention that does not materially answer the question, so it is missing_from_report; the report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT extraction is correct.", "evidence": ["card_0041", "card_0042", "card_0043"]}, {"id": "question_17", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks what targeted edits the agent made to /tmp/draft/memo.md to close the memo's fact gaps, and why. Per cards 44–49, the agent announced \"five precision edits\" (IPO background, full names for Chang/Brenner, committee chairs) and made confirmed edits adding: IPO background (Nasdaq Global Select Market, October 11, 2024, ticker GRNT, Nasdaq Rule 5610 / SOX 406 / Item 406); full names \"Victor Castellano, Managing Partner of Ridgepoint; Wei-Lin Chang, Partner of Ridgepoint\"; the chairs \"Marcus Whitfield, Lead Independent Director\" (Audit) and \"Elena Volkova\" (Nom/Gov); and \"Lars Brenner, a Clinical Research Associate in the Basel office\" with the July 14, 2024 watch details — each because the verification pass (cards 40–43) showed those required facts were missing. The report only gestures at this topic: its sequence table gives vague labels (\"Edited the memorandum's opening, the Ridgepoint and Solstice facts, the waiver question, the Brenner remediation status, and the biostatistics retainer figure\"), and section 6.2 notes separately that \"the memorandum was corrected to that title (card 49)\" for Brenner's title. Nowhere in the report (a single staged file; no other report files exist beside the entry point) do the IPO facts, the full names, the committee chairs, or the reason the edits were needed appear — grep for \"IPO\", \"October 11\", \"Victor\", \"Wei-Lin\", \"Volkova\", \"Lead Independent\", \"Nominating\" all return nothing. The vague topic mention does not materially answer the question, so this is missing_from_report. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT answer is confirmed.", "evidence": ["card_0044", "card_0045", "card_0046", "card_0047", "card_0048", "card_0049"]}, {"id": "question_18", "answer": "After the memo edits, the agent rebuilt both files (cards 51 and 55) and ran a full re-verification pass: it checked the CEO name and Code sections (cards 56-59, with Section 12's title confirmed as matching the draft resolution), reran the required-terms occurrence checks on the rebuilt text (cards 56-58), and reviewed the hotline wording (card 61). The rebuilt files remained valid Word packages, but the pass verified only term presence and structure, not legal correctness or rendered output - the agent's \"Both documents render correctly\" / \"complete and verified\" claims were overstated, as its only rendering evidence was a plain-text conversion.", "quote": "7. Rebuild and checks 54–63 Rebuilt both files after the edits (cards 51, 55). Checked the CEO name and Code sections (cards 56–59), reviewed the hotline wording (card 61), and copied source files into /app/workspace/process/ (card 63).", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The question asks what the full rebuild-and-re-verification pass after the memo edits showed. Cards 50-51 show the agent rebuilt both .docx deliverables, re-round-tripped and re-flattened them, re-ran the whole check battery plus the OOXML zip check, and the pass came back with everything OK except one residual miss: the memo still lacked the CEO's full name (\"Annika Sørensen\" MISS 0), which carried into the next step. Card 52 states the outcome in the agent's own words (\"All pass except one: the memo never spells out the CEO's full name\") and card 53 shows the fix that followed. The report communicates this materially: its sequence table says the agent \"Rebuilt both files after the edits (cards 51, 55)\" and then \"Checked the CEO name and Code sections (cards 56-59)\" (the post-fix re-verification of the name shown in card 56), and the memo-edits row reports that \"Card 52 found the CEO's full name missing; card 53 corrected it.\" A reader therefore learns that the re-verification pass passed everything except that the memo was still missing the CEO's full name, that gap carried into the next step, and was fixed and re-checked - the same substance as the gold answer, even though the report does not print the literal string \"Annika Sørensen\" as the miss (it does identify Dr. Annika Sørensen as the CEO elsewhere in the report, and card 53's edit is precisely the insertion of that name). The report-only reader's extraction is directionally right and not contradicted by the report.", "evidence": ["card_0050", "card_0051", "card_0052", "card_0053"]}, {"id": "question_19", "answer": "After the post-edit verification pass (the \"Rebuild and checks\" pass over cards 54–63), the single remaining defect was that the CEO's full name was missing from the memorandum: the check on card 52 found the CEO's full name missing, and the agent repaired it on card 53 (the \"name fix\" of the memo-edits stage).", "quote": "Card 52 found the CEO's full name missing; card 53 corrected it.", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The question asks what the single remaining defect was after the post-edit verification pass and how the agent repaired it. The report's memo-edits row (group 6, cards 44–53) states that \"Card 52 found the CEO's full name missing; card 53 corrected it,\" and its diagram labels that group \"Five edits and a name fix.\" This matches the trace exactly: card_0052 shows the agent's post-check statement \"All pass except one: the memo never spells out the CEO's full name. Quick fix, then rebuild:\", and card_0053 shows the single precision edit to /tmp/draft/memo.md. The report omits the exact replacement string (substituting \"the spouse of Dr. Annika Sørensen, Chief Executive Officer\" in the $18,000/month biostatistics-retainer paragraph), but it does communicate the material answer — the defect (CEO's full name absent from the memorandum) and the repair (the name fix in card 53, later verified via the \"Checked the CEO name\" step on card 56, and the deliverables table confirms Dr. Annika Sørensen as CEO). The report-only reader's extraction was accurate and located verbatim, so no correction to its answer is needed.", "evidence": ["card_0052", "card_0053"]}, {"id": "question_20", "answer": "The agent verified the CEO-name fix end-to-end by rebuilding both .docx files after the memo edit (cards 51, 55) and then re-running its term checks on the rebuilt, plain-text-converted documents, checking the CEO name and the Code sections across cards 56–59 (cards 56 and 58 in the required-terms occurrence-count checks). The report does not state the specific pass/fail output of that CEO-name check; the closest final check results it reports are the Section 12 title check (\"Matches.\", card 59) and the final message (card 66) calling both deliverables \"complete and verified,\" which the report itself flags as overstated.", "quote": "Card 52 found the CEO's full name missing; card 53 corrected it.", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks how the agent verified the CEO-name fix end-to-end and what the final check reported. Per card_0054 and card_0055, the agent rebuilt the memo docx (pandoc memo.md -> drafting-memorandum.docx), round-tripped it back to memo_check.txt, flattened it, and ran a final check whose output was \"valid OOXML: True\", MEMO 1 \"Annika Sørensen\", MEMO 0 \"Dr. Annika\", MEMO 1 \"spouse of\" — a check the gold answer notes culminated in a byte-level investigation of the \"Dr. Annika\" zero. The report gives none of this. It only gestures at the topic in the phase table (\"Rebuilt both files after the edits (cards 51, 55). Checked the CEO name and Code sections (cards 56–59)\") and in the checks table, which lists cards 56 and 58 only under \"Required terms present (occurrence counts)\" describing first-pass zeros for Code terms, plus \"Section 12 title matches the draft resolution ... Matches\" (card 59) — a Code-section result, not the CEO-name check outcome. Neither the verification method (round-trip of the rebuilt .docx, zipfile probe, occurrence counts) nor the final check's reported values appear anywhere in the report; searches for \"valid OOXML\", \"MEMO\", \"spouse of\", and \"Dr. Annika\" in a check context find nothing (the sole \"Dr. Annika Sørensen\" mention is the deliverables description of Code covered persons). A reader cannot learn that the fix was validated in the rebuilt .docx itself, so the answer is missing.", "evidence": ["card_0054", "card_0055"]}, {"id": "question_21", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The report does not communicate what the byte-level inspection around \"Dr. Annika Sørensen\" revealed or how the agent interpreted it. Card_0056 shows the cat -A probe exposing \"Dr.M-BM- Annika SM-CM-8rensen\" (UTF-8 U+00A0 non-breaking space after \"Dr.\", C3 B8 for ø), and card_0057 shows the agent concluding the content is correct because pandoc used a non-breaking space after \"Dr.\" (later logged as a known non-issue). The report contains none of this: no mention of a byte-level probe, non-breaking space, U+00A0, C2 A0/C3 B8, mojibake/encoding concern, or the \"Known non-issues\" logging. It gestures only at the surrounding work: \"Checked the CEO name and Code sections (cards 56–59)\" in the sequence table, and a verification row for \"Required terms present (occurrence counts)\" citing cards 56 and 58, plus the deliverable description \"covered persons Dr. Annika Sørensen (CEO)\". These are vague topic mentions of the name check, not an answer to what the byte inspection revealed or the agent's interpretation. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT is correct.", "evidence": ["card_0056", "card_0057"]}, {"id": "question_22", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The gold answer (cards 58-60) is that the final spot-check read the pandoc round-trip of the rendered output: the first 75 lines of the Code (TOC, the \"[Reserved — Message from the CEO...]\" placeholder, purpose/scope material), all of Code Section 12 (covered persons, §12.2 standards, §12.3 waivers, §12.4 designation), and the memo's opening 45 lines (banner, TO/FROM/CC/DATE/RE), concluding \"Both documents render correctly.\" The report never states what the rendered-output spot-check covered: it contains no mention of reading the first 75 lines of the Code, Section 12's content, the memo's opening lines, the banner, or the TOC/placeholder. It only quotes the agent's conclusion — \"The agent wrote \\\"Both documents render correctly\\\" (card 60) and \\\"complete and verified\\\" (card 66). Its only rendering evidence is a plain-text conversion.\" — and frames the rendering question as a limitation: \"No lawyer reviewed either document, no citation was checked against statute or rule text, and neither was viewed in a rendered form.\" Its \"spot-checks\" sentence (\"The agent made spot-checks against the source documents, covering the email's lack of a facilitation reference, the \\\"Section 5.3\\\" source, policy §4, ...\") describes a different set of source-traceability checks, not the rendered-deliverable review. Section 7's table also lumps card 58 (the head -75 spot-check) into \"Required terms present (occurrence counts).\" So the substantive coverage of the final spot-check is absent from the report; only the topic and the agent's conclusion are touched, which does not materially answer the question. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT verdict is correct.", "evidence": ["card_0058", "card_0059", "card_0060"]}, {"id": "question_23", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The question asks what the final traceability pass over the Code's riskier statements confirmed. Per card_0061 and card_0062, the agent re-read the two compliance-sensitive statements — Code §13.2 (hotline hedged \"once established\", no fabricated number) and §6.2 (facilitation payments prohibited, source-policy exception eliminated, narrow duress exception) — and concluded \"Everything checks out.\" The report covers this materially. Section 7's check table lists \"Hotline wording / 61 / Reviewed. The Code uses 'once established' and omits the reception number,\" directly confirming the hotline finding from card_0061. The sequence section states the agent \"reviewed the hotline wording (card 61),\" and the Thornberry table records the facilitation outcome as \"Prohibited in Code §6.2, with a narrow duress exception only\" against the source-policy exception — matching the §6.2 content in card_0061. The report does not quote the agent's literal \"Everything checks out\" verdict (that phrasing is not in the report, though the adjacent card_0066 verdict \"complete and verified\" is referenced elsewhere), but it conveys the same substance: both risky statements were checked and are accurate against drafting intent, so nothing needs correction. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT is a miss; the report does answer the question.", "evidence": ["card_0061", "card_0062"]}, {"id": "question_24", "answer": "The close-out was group 8 of the agent's work (\"Logs and close-out\", cards 64–66): the agent wrote a verification log (card 64) and process notes (card 65), and its final message (card 66) called both deliverables \"complete and verified\". The verification log recorded final file sizes for both documents — the Code at 33,730 bytes and the drafting memorandum at 25,710 bytes (versus 25,390 bytes at the first build) — and the workspace's process files consisted of notes, source-facts.md, verification-log.md, copies of the markdown sources, and plain-text conversions of both documents.", "quote": "8. Logs and close-out 64–66 Wrote a verification log (card 64) and process notes (card 65). The final message (card 66) calls both deliverables \"complete and verified.\"", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The report communicates the material close-out. Its group table says the agent \"copied source files into /app/workspace/process/ (card 63)\" — the archiving step the trace shows after \"Everything checks out. Finalizing the process record\" (card_0062, card_0063) — and its Deliverables table specifies what that process record holds (\"copies of the markdown sources, and plain-text conversions of both documents,\" i.e. the draft-code-source.md / draft-memo-source.md and the pandoc round-trip texts) alongside notes.md and source-facts.md, and gives the final deliverables with their final sizes, code-of-business-conduct-and-ethics.docx at 33,730 bytes and drafting-memorandum.docx at 25,710 bytes, matching the listing in card_0063. Its process-directory description matches the actual final contents (including verification-log.md, written at card 64). Gaps: the report never mentions that the agent ran a final workspace listing, does not itemize the copied file names, and omits the nine source .docx files and Calloway's .eml from its account of the final state — so the \"listing\" half is conveyed only through the deliverables/process tables rather than as a listing. But the substance of both parts (what the close-out step did, and what the final workspace contained) is trace-grounded and present, so the report answers the question.", "evidence": ["card_0062", "card_0063"]}, {"id": "question_25", "answer": "In closing out the verification phase the agent wrote a verification log (card 64, saved as verification-log.md in /app/workspace/process/), followed by process notes (card 65) and a final message (card 66) calling both deliverables \"complete and verified.\" The checks it reported as passed/confirmed fall into these categories: zip and OOXML structure of both .docx files (passed for both), plain-text conversion of both files, required terms present (occurrence counts), private matters absent from the Code, Section 12 title matching the draft resolution, and hotline wording. The report adds that the verification log's \"PASS\" entries mark term presence, not legal correctness.", "quote": "8. Logs and close-out 64–66 Wrote a verification log (card 64) and process notes (card 65). The final message (card 66) calls both deliverables \"complete and verified.\"", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The gold answer (card_0064) is that the agent closed out verification by writing /app/workspace/process/verification-log.md, a task-completion check log reporting PASS across structure/format, regulatory content, memo conventions/coverage, fact spot-checks, and a negative privacy check (0 hits for Brenner, Solstice, Henrik, Ridgepoint, Castellano, Chang, the 617 number, €4,500, the October 28 trade, Thornberry, Lindström). The report communicates this materially: section 3 identifies the close-out record (\"Wrote a verification log (card 64)\" under \"8. Logs and close-out\"), section 4 locates it as <code>verification-log.md<\/code> in <code>/app/workspace/process/<\/code> and notes its \"PASS\" entries, and section 7 (\"How far the work was checked\") enumerates the categories of checks that passed — file format/structure, plain-text conversion, required-terms presence, the negative check that private matters (Brenner, Solstice, Henrik Sørensen, Ridgepoint, the CFO trade) are absent from the Code, Section 12 title match, and hotline wording — plus fact spot-checks against the source documents. The report compresses the log's itemized PASS list (it does not recite every regulatory subcategory) but names the final record, its location, its PASS entries, and the substantive check categories including the negative privacy check the question hinges on. That is the trace-grounded answer, so the question is answerable from the report.", "evidence": ["card_0064"]}, {"id": "question_26", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "Card_0064 shows the verification log recording six specific post-build precision edits (Code cover line \"For adoption by the Board of Directors on March 18, 2025 | Effective March 18, 2025\"; IPO/Nasdaq/GRNT sentence added to the memo opening; Solstice directors' full names; Marcus Whitfield and Elena Volkova named in the waiver discussion; Lars Brenner given full name and CRO, Basel title; CEO Dr. Annika Sørensen named in memo §V.A), followed by \"Both docx files rebuilt after edits; final round-trip re-verified,\" and a \"Known non-issues\" section listing the pandoc U+00A0 after \"Dr.\" (cosmetic), the Code TOC being a live Word field, and the \"Chang\" grep hits being \"changes/change\" substrings. The report never tells the reader what the verification log recorded or classified. It only says the log was written (\"Wrote a verification log (card 64) and process notes (card 65). The final message (card 66) calls both deliverables \\\"complete and verified.\\\"\") and that \"The verification log's \\\"PASS\\\" entries mark term presence, not legal correctness.\" It separately describes some post-build edits and a rebuild in the work sequence (cards 44–55) and the \"Chang\" false positive in the checks table (card 38), but these are framed as the agent's actions, not as entries in the verification log, none of the six log-recorded edits is enumerated as such, the rebuild-and-re-verify closure attributed to the log is absent, and the U+00A0 and live-TOC non-issues do not appear at all (the report instead lists the TOC as unestablished). That is a topic mention, not an answer. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT is correct.", "evidence": ["card_0064"]}], "stages": {"spend": {"census": {"sessions": 0, "seconds": 0}, "questions": {"sessions": 0, "seconds": 0}, "answer": {"sessions": 43, "seconds": 19942}, "judge": {"sessions": 26, "seconds": 19514}, "claims": {"sessions": 110, "seconds": 30472}, "recoveries": {"sessions": 1, "seconds": 114}}, "claims": [{"id": "c1", "claim": "The agent produced two Word documents in /app/workspace/: a Code of Business Conduct and Ethics of about 8,400 words and a privileged drafting memorandum of about 4,700 words.", "type": "outcome_claim", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0029 shows the agent running pandoc to write both /app/workspace/code-of-business-conduct-and-ethics.docx and /app/workspace/drafting-memorandum.docx, and the ls output lists both files. card_0031 confirms both are genuine OOXML .docx packages (valid OOXML: True for each). card_0033 shows the pandoc plain-text round-trip word counts: 8396 words for the Code and 4653 for the memorandum, matching the claim's \"about 8,400 words\" and \"about 4,700 words.\" card_0064's verification log confirms the final deliverables in /app/workspace (33,730 and 25,710 bytes), the ~8,400/~4,700 word round-trips, and the memo's privileged-banner legal-memo conventions (TO/FROM/RE, \"privileged\" formatting)."}, {"id": "c2", "claim": "The drafting memorandum is written as from a named partner at Whitfield &amp; Crane LLP to the company's General Counsel.", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0026 (the Write of memo.md, the drafting memorandum) shows the memo header block: \"TO: James Calloway, General Counsel and Corporate Secretary, Greenfield Therapeutics, Inc.\" and \"FROM: Sarah L[indström, Partner] ... Whitfield & Crane ... Corporate/Securities — Boston\", with CC to David Okafor, Whitfield & Crane LLP, and a closing \"Respectfully submitted, Whitfield & Crane LLP By: Sarah Lindström, Partner\". card_0043's grep of the flattened memo confirms the same: \"Sarah Lindström, Partner, Whitfield [& Crane] ... David Okafor, Whitfield & Crane LLP ... Respectfully submitted, Whitfield & Crane LLP By: Sarah Lindstr...\". Both elements of the claim hold: a named partner (Sarah Lindström) … [87 more characters]"}, {"id": "c3", "claim": "Both files are valid Word packages.", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0031 shows a python zipfile check on both deliverables (code-of-business-conduct-and-ethics.docx and drafting-memorandum.docx) printing \"valid OOXML docx: True\" for each, with [Content_Types].xml and word/document.xml present and non-trivial document.xml sizes (119,378 and 92,175 bytes). card_0032 shows the agent immediately concluding \"Both are valid.\" card_0064's written verification log records the same result: \"Both are valid OOXML packages (zip; [Content_Types].xml + word/document.xml present).\" A valid OOXML zip package with word/document.xml is what makes a .docx a valid Word package, so the claim holds as stated."}, {"id": "c4", "claim": "The agent's string checks found the required terms in the documents.", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The cards show the agent ran grep-based string checks over the two documents and\nthat the required terms were found. card_0034 greps ~54 required terms in\ncode_check.txt and shows counts of 1+ for nearly all (e.g. Nasdaq Listing Rule\n5610, Sarbanes-Oxley, Section 406, Dodd-Frank, 10b5-1); card_0036 re-checks the\nfew apparent zero-hits after flattening newlines and they are all found\n(1x each for Item 406 of Regulation S-K, pre-approved, the \"full, fair,\naccurate, timely, and understandable\" phrase, GDPR, National Labor Relations).\ncard_0040 greps the memo's required facts with counts of 1 for Brenner,\nSolstice, Henrik, Ridgepoint, Castellano and the dates/figures; card_0042\nshows the remaini … [277 more characters]"}, {"id": "c5", "claim": "No lawyer reviewed either document.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The cards show both deliverables left in draft form with lawyer review still outstanding, and no lawyer review occurs anywhere in the trace. card_0006 (Calloway's drafting instructions) sets the timeline: drafts due by Feb 28, then \"Week of March 3: Calloway reviews and provides comments; may circulate to a couple of Board members\" — the GC (a lawyer) review is a future step after delivery. card_0028 shows the Code cover deliberately changed to \"For adoption by the Board of Directors on March 18, 2025\" because it is the Feb 28 draft, and card_0064's verification log notes only the agent's own checks (pandoc round-trips, automated content checks) with a CEO-letter placeholder still reserved. … [422 more characters]"}, {"id": "c6", "claim": "No citation in the documents was checked against statute or rule text.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The claim is that no citation in the deliverables was checked against statute or\nrule text. The saved trace shows the regulatory citations were grounded only in\nthe local source documents and self-consistency, never against external\nstatute/rule text: card_0022 (fact inventory) derives the legal parameters\n(Nasdaq 5610, SOX 406/Item 406, Item 404, 8-K Item 5.05, SOX 806/21F, FCPA,\nrevDSG/GDPR/21 CFR Part 11, PhRMA/EFPIA) as a summary of the provided company\nsources; card_0034 shows the only \"required elements\" verification as grep\noccurrence counts of citation strings in the pandoc round-trip text of the\ndraft itself; and card_0064 (the written verification log) describes every\nregulatory co … [217 more characters]"}, {"id": "c7", "claim": "Neither document was viewed in a rendered form.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "A grep over all 66 cards for render/pdf/soffice/libreoffice/screenshot/png/html/preview found no rendering step anywhere in the trajectory. The only inspections of the two deliverable docx files were: pandoc build and `file` listing (card_0029), a Python zipfile check for OOXML structure (card_0031), and pandoc -t plain round-trip text plus grep-based content checks (card_0033). The verification log written in card_0064 records exactly these as the file-format checks (\"pandoc <docx> -t plain round-trips cleanly\") and explicitly notes the Code TOC only populates on open in Word — evidence the agent never opened the documents in a rendered viewer."}, {"id": "c8", "claim": "The Code is a working draft for counsel review and is not ready for adoption.", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "unsupported_or_contradicted", "why": "The claim says the Code is \"a working draft for counsel review\" and \"not ready for adoption.\" The cards show the opposite: card_0066 (final answer) describes the delivered code-of-business-conduct-and-ethics.docx as a \"Board-ready Code ... in form for adoption at the March 18, 2025 Board meeting as Exhibit A to the circulated draft resolution\"; card_0065 (process notes) likewise calls it a \"Board-ready code consolidating the 10 source policies + addressing all flagged gaps\" with status COMPLETE. card_0028 shows the agent edited the cover date from \"Adopted by the Board of Directors\" to \"For adoption by the Board of Directors on March 18, 2025,\" which is consistent with a draft prepared for a … [191 more characters]"}, {"id": "c9", "claim": "The draft board resolution was not edited or finalized.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0010 shows the agent's only interaction with the draft board resolution was a Read of the pandoc-extracted copy, used as a structural constraint (the §12 SFO-Code designation). No Write or Edit command anywhere in the 66 cards targets a board-resolution file; all edits (e.g., the 6 verification edits logged in card_0064) went to /tmp/draft/code.md and /tmp/draft/memo.md. card_0001 and card_0063 bracket the run: the final workspace listing still shows draft-board-resolution.docx at its original 42,092 bytes and Jun 23 16:02 timestamp, i.e., the source document was left untouched — not edited or finalized."}, {"id": "c10", "claim": "The memorandum says the draft board resolution needs no changes, but the record does not support that conclusion.", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "Card 1 of the claim — the memo says the draft board resolution needs no changes — is shown by card_0026, the memo's written content: §IX says \"(2) finalize the Adoption Resolution (no changes currently anticipated — the draft aligns)\" and §VIII.3 states \"the draft is in form for the Adoption Resolution as circulated (Section 12 designation, supersession list, and waiver resolution all align).\" Card 2 — that the record does not support that conclusion — is also shown by the sources. card_0006 (Calloway's Jan 27, 2025 instruction email) lists the draft board resolution as \"authorizing adoption of the Code. I'd like you to finalize this once the Code is in shape,\" i.e., the client asked for it … [1,009 more characters]"}, {"id": "c11", "claim": "The memorandum attributes a \"Section 5.3\" reference to the client's instructions, when the reference comes from the Thornberry report and the client's email does not contain it.", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0026 shows the drafting memorandum (memo §VI.3) stating \"Your instructions cite the facilitation-payments exception as 'Section 5.3' of the Anti-Corruption Policy; in the policy as provided it appears at Section 4\" — i.e., the memo attributes the \"Section 5.3\" reference to the client's instructions. card_0008 (full text, line 1306) shows the Thornberry readiness assessment itself referring to \"Section 5.3 of the Anti-Corruption Compliance Policy (April 3, 2022)\" for the facilitation-payments exception, so the reference comes from the Thornberry report. card_0006's full email text contains no occurrence of \"facilitation\" and no \"5.3\"/\"Section 5.3\" match, so the client's email does not co … [20 more characters]"}, {"id": "c12", "claim": "Most regulatory citations in the memorandum come from the agent's own knowledge, not from any document in the record.", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "unsupported_or_contradicted", "why": "I enumerated the regulatory citations in the memorandum (card_0026, ~53 citation instances): Item 404 (13), Item 406/Reg S-K (9), Form 10-K (5), 10b5-1 (4), SOX 406 (4), Item 5.05/8-K (4), Section 16 (2), Item 16 (2), Nasdaq 5610, SOX 301, SOX 806, Dodd-Frank 21F, revDSG, GDPR, HIPAA, 21 CFR Part 11, PhRMA Code, EFPIA Code, Form 4 (1 each). Then I grepped every record document (cards 0006, 0008, 0010, 0011, 0013, 0014, 0016, 0017, 0019, 0020, plus the memo-edit cards). Almost all of these citations are present in the record: the Calloway email (card_0006) cites Nasdaq 5610, SOX 406, Item 406, Item 404, Item 5.05 of 8-K, and 10b5-1; the Thornberry assessment (card_0008) cites SOX 806, Dodd-Fr … [756 more characters]"}, {"id": "c13", "claim": "The memorandum's outstanding-items table lists 15 actions.", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0026 (the Write of /tmp/draft/memo.md, turn 31) contains the memorandum's\n\"VII. Outstanding Items and Action Items\" table with exactly 15 numbered action\nrows (1. hotline placeholder removal, 2. hotline vendor, 3. Brenner remedial\nreview, 4. Sørensen arrangement, 5. Solstice contract, 6. RPT inventory, 7.\nInsider Trading Policy amendment, 8. training program, 9. Data Privacy Policy,\n10. CCO hire, 11. Code distribution/acknowledgments, 12. posting/Exhibit 14,\n13. CEO letter, 14. conforming amendments, 15. Swiss-law supplement). Later\nmemo edits (cards 0045-0053) do not modify that table, so the final memo's\noutstanding-items table lists 15 actions, as claimed."}, {"id": "c14", "claim": "The record shows none of the 15 outstanding actions as completed.", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0026 shows the memo's §VII \"Outstanding Items and Action Items\" written as \"The following remain open, with suggested owners and timing,\" followed by a 15-row table (items 1–15) where every row carries a suggested owner and a future target (Immediate, FY2025 Q2, Before Code rollout, Q2 2025, etc.) and none is marked completed. card_0064's verification log confirms the record contains exactly a \"15-row action table with owners/targets (memo §VII)\" with no completion status on any item, and card_0066's final summary likewise describes only a \"15-item outstanding-actions table with owners and targets.\" The later edits in the trace (e.g., card_0049) modified other memo sections, not the sta … [95 more characters]"}, {"id": "c15", "claim": "The client's request is the January 27, 2025 email from James Calloway, General Counsel and Corporate Secretary, to Sarah Lindström of Whitfield &amp; Crane LLP (card 6).", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0006 shows the agent reading /app/workspace/calloway-drafting-instructions.eml: From: James Calloway <jcalloway@greenfieldtx.com>, To: Sarah Lindström <slindstrom@whitfieldcrane.com>, Date: Mon, 27 Jan 2025, with the signature block \"James Calloway / General Counsel & Corporate Secretary, Greenfield Therapeutics, Inc.\" and content that is the drafting instructions for the Code of Business Conduct and Ethics (the client's request). card_0005 corroborates that the task's notes identify calloway-drafting-instructions.eml as the drafting instructions to read first, i.e., the client's request. All details in the claim (sender, title, recipient and firm, January 27, 2025 date) match."}, {"id": "c16", "claim": "The request asks for a single Code satisfying Nasdaq Listing Rule 5610 and SOX 406 (Item 406 of Regulation S-K), organized by topic, with a CEO letter placeholder, applying globally with a local-law savings clause, and to be posted publicly and filed as Exhibit 14 to the FY2024 Form 10-K.", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0006 shows the full calloway-drafting-instructions.eml, the request the claim summarizes. It asks for \"a single, unified Code of Business Conduct and Ethics that satisfies Nasdaq Listing Rule 5610\" that \"must also satisfy SOX Section 406 requirements\" as defined under \"SOX Section 406 and Item 406 of Reg S-K\"; §IV of the email asks that the Code be \"organized by topic area,\" include \"a placeholder for a 'Message from the CEO' / 'Letter from Leadership',\" \"apply globally\" with a note on additional local-law requirements \"for now a savings clause will do,\" and be \"posted on the company website and filed as Exhibit 14 to the Form 10-K.\" The email does not say \"FY2024\" explicitly, but the r … [248 more characters]"}, {"id": "c17", "claim": "The request asks for tiered related-party transaction rules: the existing $5,000 internal disclosure threshold stays, and transactions of $120,000 or more (Item 404) escalate to the Audit Committee.", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0006 (Calloway drafting-instructions email, read in full via cards/full/card_0006.txt) contains the exact request: \"What I'd like is a tiered approach: broader conflicts are disclosed internally at a reasonable threshold (we can keep $5,000 or adjust it modestly), but any transaction meeting the $120,000 Item 404 threshold gets escalated to the Audit Committee for review and approval. Please build that architecture into the Code.\" The claim's characterization that the $5,000 internal disclosure threshold \"stays\" is a fair reading of \"we can keep $5,000 or adjust it modestly\" plus the statement that he \"don't want to raise the internal disclosure threshold,\" and the final record in card_ … [132 more characters]"}, {"id": "c18", "claim": "The request asks for insider-trading rules covering pre-clearance, blackout periods, and Rule 10b5-1 plans.", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0006 shows the agent reading the request (calloway-drafting-instructions.eml), whose \"3. Insider Trading Concerns\" section asks the drafter to strengthen the insider trading provisions with a pre-clearance requirement for officers and directors and to address blackout periods and 10b5-1 trading plans — exactly the three topics the claim attributes to the request."}, {"id": "c19", "claim": "The request asks for a waiver procedure with Board or committee approval and disclosure within four business days (Form 8-K Item 5.05).", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0006 shows the agent reading the drafting instructions (calloway-drafting-instructions.eml), whose \"4. Waiver Procedure\" section requires the Code to include a waiver procedure in which any waiver for an executive officer or director is approved by the Board of Directors or the appropriate Board committee (Audit or Nom/Gov) and disclosed within four business days under Item 5.05 of Form 8-K (8-K or website) — exactly what the claim states. card_0022's source-fact inventory independently records the same requirement (\"Waivers: Board or (if delegated) Audit Committee; 8-K 5.05 4 business days and/or website\")."}, {"id": "c20", "claim": "The request asks for responses to all 14 findings in the Thornberry Compliance Readiness Assessment (June 28, 2024).", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0006 shows the Calloway drafting-instructions email (the \"request\") instructing the drafter to \"address all of the Thornberry findings\" and, under \"5. Thornberry Findings — General Instruction,\" referencing \"the 14 deficiency areas\" identified in the Thornberry report. card_0008 shows the Thornberry Compliance Readiness Assessment itself: \"Report Date: June 28, 2024,\" with a Summary of Findings table and \"Of the 14 findings, four (4) are rated...\" — confirming the report has exactly 14 findings and the stated date. Together they support the claim that the request asks for responses to all 14 findings in that June 28, 2024 assessment."}, {"id": "c21", "claim": "The request asks for designation of Calloway as interim compliance officer, with a Chief Compliance Officer hire planned for Q2 2025.", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0006 shows the actual request — Calloway's Jan 27, 2025 drafting-instructions email — which states the company is \"budgeting to hire a Chief Compliance Officer by Q2 2025\" and that the Code \"should designate me as interim compliance officer and build in a structure that will accommodate the CCO once we have one.\" That matches the claim exactly (designate Calloway as interim compliance officer; CCO hire planned for Q2 2025). card_0066 corroborates that the deliverable reflected this request (\"Calloway named Interim Compliance Officer with the CCO role built in for Q2 2025\")."}, {"id": "c22", "claim": "The request asks for a companion drafting memorandum and finalization of the draft board resolution \"once the Code is in shape.\"", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0006 (the read of /app/workspace/calloway-drafting-instructions.eml, the\nrequesting client's drafting-instructions email) shows both elements of the\nclaim in full. Its source-materials list item 9 reads: \"Draft Board Resolution\n(draft-board-resolution.docx) — authorizing adoption of the Code. I'd like you\nto finalize this once the Code is in shape.\" — matching the claimed \"once the\nCode is in shape\" quote. Its structure/format section separately says: \"please\nprepare a companion drafting memorandum that identifies all issues found in the\nexisting policies, how each is resolved in the Code, and any remaining action\nitems.\" The request thus does ask for both a companion drafting memorandu … [75 more characters]"}, {"id": "c23", "claim": "The email's timeline: draft by February 28, 2025; board pre-meeting March 10, 2025; adoption March 18, 2025.", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0006 shows the read of calloway-drafting-instructions.eml, whose timeline\ntable states: February 28, 2025 — first draft of Code and drafting memorandum\nto Calloway for review; March 10, 2025 — final draft circulated to full Board\nas part of pre-meeting materials; March 18, 2025 — Board meeting, adoption of\nCode by resolution. Each date and milestone in the claim corresponds exactly\nto the email."}, {"id": "c24", "claim": "The FY2025 compliance budget is $850,000.", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0022 (source fact inventory) records \"## Budget (Calloway): FY2025 compliance $850,000 = training $120k + hotline $85k + CCO $275–325k + monitoring\", and card_0026 (the drafting memorandum) states the Code is \"implementable within the **$850,000 FY2025 compliance budget** (training $120,000; hotline $85,000; CCO $275,000–$3…). Both match the claim exactly: the FY2025 compliance budget is $850,000."}, {"id": "c25", "claim": "The report's final message (card 66) calls both deliverables \"complete and verified.\"", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0066 is the final message of the trajectory (turn 82, last of 66 cards, empty command, assistant text) and its first line reads \"Both deliverables are complete and verified.\" — exactly the phrasing the claim attributes to it. The message then details both deliverables (code-of-business-conduct-and-ethics.docx and drafting-memorandum.docx) and a verification summary, consistent with the claim."}, {"id": "c26", "claim": "The agent listed the workspace and checked that pandoc 2.17.1.1 was available.", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0001 shows the agent ran `ls -la /app/workspace/` and received the workspace directory listing (docx policy files, .eml, process dir). card_0002 shows `which pandoc python3 && pandoc --version | head -2` returning `/usr/bin/pandoc` and `pandoc 2.17.1.1`, confirming pandoc 2.17.1.1 was checked and available, exactly as the claim states."}, {"id": "c27", "claim": "The agent extracted the source .docx policies to markdown in /tmp/extract.", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0003 shows the agent running `mkdir -p /tmp/extract && for f in *.docx; do pandoc \"$f\" -t markdown -o \"/tmp/extract/${f%.docx}.md\"; done` in /app/workspace, and the tool output lists nine extracted .md policy files (e.g., anti-corruption-policy.md, insider-trading-policy.md, whistleblower-policy.md) in /tmp/extract. Later cards (card_0008, card_0010, etc.) read those /tmp/extract/*.md files, confirming the extraction happened as stated."}, {"id": "c28", "claim": "The agent wrote process notes (card 5).", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0005 (the 5th card) shows a Write command creating \"Process notes — Code of Business Conduct & Ethics (biopharma IPO)\" with task, sources, and a status log, i.e. the agent writing process notes exactly as the claim states."}, {"id": "c29", "claim": "The agent read the instruction email (card 6), the Thornberry assessment (card 8), the draft board resolution (card 10), the Audit Committee Charter (card 11), and six policy documents (cards 13, 14, 16, 17, 19, 20).", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "Each cited card is a Read of the document the claim names, with matching content: card_0006 reads calloway-drafting-instructions.eml (the instruction email), card_0008 reads the Thornberry compliance readiness assessment, card_0010 reads the draft board resolution, card_0011 reads the Audit Committee Charter, and cards 0013, 0014, 0016, 0017, 0019, 0020 read the conflict-of-interest, insider-trading, anti-corruption, whistleblower, social-media policies and the employee handbook ethics extract — six policy documents, exactly as claimed."}, {"id": "c30", "claim": "The Thornberry assessment covers April 15 to June 21, 2024.", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0008 (Read of /tmp/extract/thornberry-readiness-assessment.md, full text in cards/full/card_0008.txt) shows the Thornberry report stating \"The assessment was conducted over the period of April 15, 2024 through June 21, 2024,\" and its phase timeline (Phase 1 April 15–30 through Phase 5 June 18–21, 2024) matches. The report passage is a faithful restatement of the source."}, {"id": "c31", "claim": "The agent wrote process/source-facts.md, covering people, figures, the 14 findings, and source discrepancies.", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0022 shows the Write to /app/workspace/process/source-facts.md succeeding (\"File created successfully at: /app/workspace/process/source-facts.md\"). The written content contains a \"## People\" section (Calloway, Sørensen, Dominguez, Whitfield, Volkova, etc.), figures (revenue ~$14.2M, ~410 employees, $850,000 FY2025 compliance budget, $216,000/yr retainer, $120,000 Item 404 threshold, ~14.7% Ridgepoint stake), a numbered \"The 14 Thornberry findings\" section listing all 14 findings, and a \"Source discrepancies found\" section (225 vs 235 Binney, \"rare genetic\" vs \"rare autoimmune\", facilitation-exception section citation, hotline hours, etc.). Each element the claim names — people, figures, … [72 more characters]"}, {"id": "c32", "claim": "The source-facts note contains errors.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 2, "verdict": "unsupported_or_contradicted", "why": "The claim says the source-facts note contains errors (as unresolved work). The\ntrace shows this work in enough detail that such errors would appear, and they\ndo not. card_0022 carries the full text of process/source-facts.md: its\n\"Source discrepancies found\" section lists errors in the underlying source\ndocuments (225 vs 235 Binney, rare genetic vs autoimmune, the §5.3-vs-§4\ncitation, stale headcount, report dating, hotline hours), each resolved with an\nexplicit \"use X\" decision — it documents discrepancies in sources, and never\nflags an error in the note itself. card_0064's verification log records the\nfact-traceability pass and per-fact spot-checks on exactly those inventory\nfacts as all P … [432 more characters]"}, {"id": "c33", "claim": "The agent drafted the Code in markdown at /tmp/draft/code.md, with 15 numbered sections plus an appendix, and the memorandum at /tmp/draft/memo.md (card 26).", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0024 shows the agent writing the Code markdown via Write to file_path /tmp/draft/code.md; its content contains numbered sections 1 through 15 (1. Purpose, Scope, and Applicability ... 15. Acknowledgment, Questions, and Public Availability) followed by \"Appendix A — Acknowledgment of the Code of Business Conduct and Ethics\", i.e. 15 numbered sections plus an appendix. card_0026 (the card the claim cites) shows the Write of the DRAFTING MEMORANDUM to file_path /tmp/draft/memo.md, and its own text states the Code has \"Fifteen numbered sections plus an acknowledgment appendix (Appendix A)\". Both file paths and the structural detail match the claim as stated."}, {"id": "c34", "claim": "The agent changed the Code's date line to adoption on March 18, 2025 (card 28).", "type": "decision", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0028 shows the Edit to /tmp/draft/code.md replacing the date line \"Adopted by the Board of Directors | Effective March 18, 2025\" with \"For adoption by the Board of Directors on March 18, 2025 | Effective March 18, 2025\", exactly the change the claim describes. card_0027 shows the agent stating this was a deliberate consistency refinement (the cover line should say \"for adoption\") before the edit."}, {"id": "c35", "claim": "The agent built both .docx files with pandoc (card 29).", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0029 (turn 35) shows the exact claimed action: `pandoc code.md -f markdown -t docx --toc --toc-depth=1 -o /app/workspace/code-of-business-conduct-and-ethics.docx && pandoc memo.md -f markdown -t docx -o /app/workspace/drafting-memorandum.docx`. The following `ls -la` lists both output .docx files with fresh Oct 5 04:35 timestamps (all pre-existing source .docx files are dated Jun 23), confirming pandoc actually produced both files; the exit code 127 came from the trailing `file` command, not from pandoc. card_0051 (turn 63) and card_0055 (turn 67) corroborate by rebuilding the same two files with pandoc and verifying both are valid OOXML docx archives."}, {"id": "c36", "claim": "The build chain exited with code 127 because a command called file, which is not installed.", "type": "runtime_failure", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0029 shows the build chain (pandoc builds of code.md and memo.md, ls, then `file` on the two docx outputs) ending with \"Exit code 127\" and the stderr line \"/bin/bash: line 1: file: command not found\", i.e., the chain failed at the `file` command because it is not installed, exactly as claimed."}, {"id": "c37", "claim": "Despite the failed build chain, both .docx files were written.", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0029 shows the build chain (pandoc code.md -> code-of-business-conduct-and-ethics.docx && pandoc memo.md -> drafting-memorandum.docx && ls -la && file ...) exiting with code 127 — the failure was only the trailing `file: command not found` — while the `ls` within that same chain lists both deliverables with fresh Oct 5 04:35 timestamps (33,730 and 25,390 bytes, versus Jun 23 for the untouched source docx files), proving both writes succeeded. card_0031 then opens both files and confirms each is a valid OOXML docx with a non-trivial word/document.xml, corroborating that both were genuinely written despite the failed command chain."}, {"id": "c38", "claim": "The agent checked the zip structure (card 31) and converted both files to plain text (card 33).", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0031 shows the agent running a python zipfile script that opens both code-of-business-conduct-and-ethics.docx and drafting-memorandum.docx, lists their zip parts, and validates OOXML structure ([Content_Types].xml and word/document.xml present, 16 parts each) — i.e., checking the zip structure. card_0033 shows pandoc converting both docx files with -t plain into code_check.txt and memo_check.txt (8396 and 4653 words respectively) — i.e., converting both files to plain text."}, {"id": "c39", "claim": "The first term checks returned zeros for several required terms (card 34).", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0034 shows the first required-terms loop (grep -c over code_check.txt) returning 0 for several terms, including \"Item 406 of Regulation S-K\", \"pre-approval\", \"full, fair, accurate, timely, and understandable\", and \"General Data Protection Regulation\", while other terms returned nonzero counts. card_0035 confirms this was the first check, with the assistant noting the zeros and re-checking with normalized whitespace (the later re-check is card_0036)."}, {"id": "c40", "claim": "The agent reran the term checks on flattened text (card 36).", "type": "recovery", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0034 shows the initial term checks grepping required elements against code_check.txt (plain-text extraction), where several terms scored 0. card_0035 records the agent attributing the zeros to line-wrap artifacts and deciding to \"re-check with normalized whitespace.\" card_0036 then flattens code_check.txt and memo_check.txt into *_flat.txt via tr '\\n' ' ' and reruns the term checks (grep -o ... wc -l) on code_flat.txt, with the previously zero terms (\"Item 406 of Regulation S-K\", \"full, fair, accurate, timely, and understandable\", \"General Data Protection Regulation\", etc.) now returning 1 — exactly the claim that the agent reran the term checks on flattened text (card 36)."}, {"id": "c41", "claim": "The rerun term checks led to the report that private matters were absent from the Code (card 37).", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The \"rerun term checks\" are the whitespace-normalized re-checks: card_0036 rebuilt code_flat.txt from code_check.txt after card_0034/0035 suspected line-wrap artifacts, and card_0037 then ran the \"CODE must NOT contain (private matters)\" term sweep over code_flat.txt (Brenner, Solstice, Ridgepoint, Whitfield, phone numbers, share counts, etc.), showing zero hits for all private matters except false-positive \"Chang\"/\"Calloway\" hits. card_0038 showed the \"Chang\" hits were only the word \"changes\" and \"Calloway\" is James Calloway, the named General Counsel, and card_0039 states \"The Code is clean.\" So the rerun checks indeed led to the reported conclusion that private matters were absent from th … [28 more characters]"}, {"id": "c42", "claim": "The agent edited the memorandum's opening, the Ridgepoint and Solstice facts, the waiver question, the Brenner remediation status, and the biostatistics retainer figure.", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "Each named memo edit appears as an Edit to /tmp/draft/memo.md in the trace, and card_0064's verification log enumerates the same six edits. card_0045: opening of the memo expanded with the IPO/Nasdaq Global Select/GRNT background sentence. card_0046: the Solstice Data Analytics contract facts — a Ridgepoint Capital Partners portfolio company — updated with directors' full names (Victor Castellano, Managing Partner; Wei-Lin Chang, Partner). card_0048: the waiver-authority question paragraph edited to name committee chairs (Marcus Whitfield, Lead Independent Director; Elena Volkova). card_0049: the Brenner remediation \"Outstanding\" item expanded with full name, title, and office (Lars Brenner, … [424 more characters]"}, {"id": "c43", "claim": "A check (card 52) found the CEO's full name missing from the deliverables.", "type": "runtime_failure", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "Card_0051's deliverable-check command grep-scans the rebuilt docx files for required strings and its full output shows \"MISS 0  Annika Sørensen\" in both the CODE and MEMO required-string sections — the CEO's full name was absent from the deliverables. Card_0052 (the step the claim labels \"card 52\") is the assistant's failure-recovery observation: \"All pass except one: the memo never spells out the CEO's full name.\" Card_0053 then shows the fix, editing memo.md to spell out \"Dr. Annika Sørensen, Chief Executive Officer\". The claim's finding that the check caught the CEO's full name missing from the deliverables is exactly what the cards show."}, {"id": "c44", "claim": "The agent corrected the missing CEO's full name (card 53).", "type": "recovery", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0052 shows the agent flagging that \"the memo never spells out the CEO's full name\" and deciding to fix it; card_0053 shows the actual Edit to /tmp/draft/memo.md replacing \"the CEO's spouse\" with \"the spouse of Dr. Annika Sørensen, Chief Executive Officer\", which is exactly the missing-CEO-full-name correction the claim attributes to card 53."}, {"id": "c45", "claim": "The agent rebuilt both files after the edits (cards 51, 55).", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0051 (turn 63) runs pandoc to rebuild both deliverables from the edited markdown sources — code.md → code-of-business-conduct-and-ethics.docx and memo.md → drafting-memorandum.docx — after the earlier code.md edit (card_0028) and memo edits (cards 0045–0049), with both docx files validating as OOXML. card_0055 (turn 67) then rebuilds drafting-memorandum.docx again after the final memo edit (card_0053, turn 65), and no further edits to either source file occur afterwards (cards 0056–0066 are inspection/copy/writes of logs only). So both files were rebuilt after the edits, as the claim states."}, {"id": "c46", "claim": "The agent checked the CEO name and Code sections (cards 56–59) and reviewed the hotline wording (card 61).", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0056 shows the agent grepping for \"Annika\" to verify the CEO name (Dr. Annika Sørensen, Chief Executive Officer) in the rendered memo, with card_0057 recording the agent's conclusion that the content is correct. card_0058 and card_0059 show spot-checks of Code sections (first 75 lines including the CEO message, and Section 12 Supplemental Code of Ethics). card_0061 shows the explicit \"hotline phrasing check\" of Code §13.2. Each cited card matches the claim as stated."}, {"id": "c47", "claim": "The agent copied source files into /app/workspace/process/ (card 63).", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0063 (turn 77) shows the agent running `cp /tmp/draft/code.md /app/workspace/process/draft-code-source.md && cp /tmp/draft/memo.md /app/workspace/process/draft-memo-source.md && cp ... code-pandoc-roundtrip.txt ... memo-pandoc-roundtrip.txt`, followed by an `ls -la` of /app/workspace/process/. The files copied include the source markdown files (code.md, memo.md), matching the claim that source files were copied into /app/workspace/process/."}, {"id": "c48", "claim": "The agent wrote a verification log (card 64) and process notes (card 65).", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0064 (turn 79) is a Write command whose content begins \"# Verification log (task completion check)\" — the agent writing a verification log, exactly as the claim states. card_0065 (turn 81) is a Write command to /app/workspace/process/notes.md with content \"# Process notes — Code of Business Conduct & Ethics (biopharma IPO)\", which the tool confirmed was updated successfully — the agent writing process notes. Both events in the claim appear in the trace as stated."}, {"id": "c49", "claim": "Both .docx files were written.", "type": "other", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0029 shows pandoc writing both /app/workspace/code-of-business-conduct-and-ethics.docx and /app/workspace/drafting-memorandum.docx, and ls -la listing both with fresh Oct 5 04:35 timestamps (the exit 127 came from the trailing `file` command, not the pandoc writes). card_0031 confirms both are valid OOXML docx with non-trivial document.xml. After the late edits, card_0051 rebuilds both files and verifies both as valid OOXML, and card_0055 rebuilds drafting-memorandum.docx after the final fix, again verifying valid OOXML: True. So both .docx files were written as claimed."}, {"id": "c50", "claim": "The memorandum's facilitation-payments cross-reference says the client's instructions cite the exception as \"Section 5.3\" of the Anti-Corruption Policy.", "type": "other", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0026 is the written drafting memo (/tmp/draft/memo.md) and its source-discrepancy log contains item 3, \"Facilitation-payments cross-reference\": \"Your instructions cite the facilitation-payments exception as 'Section 5.3' of the Anti-Corruption Policy; in the policy as provided it appears at Section 4 (with the pre-approval form at Appendix B).\" That is exactly what the claim states. card_0022 (the fact inventory) corroborates the underlying fact: \"Calloway email cites facilitation exception as 'Section 5.3' of Anti-Corruption Policy; actual policy §4 → cite §4 in memo.\""}, {"id": "c51", "claim": "The January 27 email has no facilitation reference.", "type": "other", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0006 shows the agent reading /app/workspace/calloway-drafting-instructions.eml (Date: Mon, 27 Jan 2025), and its evidence preserves the entire 295-line email. A grep for \"facil\" over the full card text returns no matches, and the email's anti-corruption discussion covers only the HCP/Brenner gap and the compliance-officer role — no facilitation-payment reference appears anywhere in the message. The claim that the January 27 email has no facilitation reference is therefore borne out by the saved source."}, {"id": "c52", "claim": "The \"Section 5.3\" wording appears in the Thornberry report, Finding 6.", "type": "other", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0008 is the agent's read of /tmp/extract/thornberry-readiness-assessment.md. The full trace shows the section \"**[IV.F. Finding 6: Facilitation Payments Exception Creates International Legal Exposure --- HIGH]**\" whose description reads \"Section 5.3 of the Anti-Corruption Compliance Policy (April 3, 2022) contains an exception permitting 'facilitation payments'...\", so the \"Section 5.3\" wording does appear in the Thornberry report's Finding 6, as the claim states."}, {"id": "c53", "claim": "The underlying point is correct: the April 3, 2022 policy places the exception in §4, which allows $200 per payment and $500 per agency per year.", "type": "other", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0016 reads the Anti-Corruption Compliance Policy, which is dated \"Adopted: April 3, 2022.\" Its Section 4 (Facilitation Payments) sets the exception thresholds: facilitation payments \"may not exceed $200 per payment and $500 in the aggregate to any single government agency or department in any calendar year.\" This matches the claim exactly — the April 3, 2022 policy, the exception located in §4, and the $200 per payment / $500 per agency per year limits."}, {"id": "c54", "claim": "The fact inventory repeats the \"Section 5.3\" misattribution, and nothing in the record corrects it.", "type": "other", "section": 4, "verdict": "unsupported_or_contradicted", "why": "The claim says the fact inventory (card 22) repeats the \"Section 5.3\"\nmisattribution and that nothing in the record corrects it. Both halves are\ncontradicted. card_0022's fact inventory does contain the string \"Section\n5.3\", but only inside its \"Source discrepancies found\" section, where it\nexplicitly flags the error: \"Calloway email cites facilitation exception as\n'Section 5.3' of Anti-Corruption Policy; actual policy §4 → cite §4 in\nmemo.\" That is an identification of the misattribution, not a repetition of\nit as correct. And the record does correct it: card_0026 (the drafting\nmemo) states the facilitation-payments exception appears at Section 4 of the\nAnti-Corruption Policy (pre-approval … [283 more characters]"}, {"id": "c55", "claim": "The fact inventory calls Lars Brenner a \"CRO,\" while the Thornberry report says Clinical Research Associate.", "type": "other", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0022 shows the fact inventory (source-facts.md, \"## People\" section) listing \"Lars Brenner — CRO, Basel\", and card_0008 shows the Thornberry readiness assessment's Appendix B interview table giving Lars Brenner's title as \"Clinical Research Associate\" (Basel, May 20, 2024), so the report's stated discrepancy between the two sources is accurate as the evidence records it."}, {"id": "c56", "claim": "The memorandum was corrected to the title Clinical Research Associate for Lars Brenner.", "type": "other", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0049 (turn 61) shows an Edit to /tmp/draft/memo.md replacing \"the Brenner incident\" with \"the incident involving Lars Brenner, a Clinical Research Associate in the Basel office\", and the tool confirmed the file was updated. This is the memorandum being corrected to the title Clinical Research Associate for Lars Brenner, exactly as the report passage (citing card 49) states."}, {"id": "c57", "claim": "The new drafts use the 235 Binney Street address.", "type": "decision", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0022 (source fact inventory) records the decision: \"Address: 235 Binney Street, Suite 400, Cambridge, MA 02142 (Jan 2025 email, Sep 2024 Charter, Mar 2025 resolution). Legacy policies (2019–2024) all say 225 Binney Street → discrepancy; use 235 in new Code, flag legacy updates.\" card_0026 (the drafting memorandum write) shows the new draft carrying that decision: \"the legacy policies (2019–2024) and the Thornberry cover page give 225 Binney Street; the Audit Committee Charter (Sept. 2024), the Adoption Resolution, and your current signature block give 235 Binney Street, Suite 400. We used 235 Binney Street (the more...)\" and the outstanding-items table lists \"legacy address corrections … [363 more characters]"}, {"id": "c58", "claim": "The legacy policies give the office address as 225 Binney Street.", "type": "other", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The legacy policy documents read in the trace show the office address as 225 Binney Street: the anti-corruption compliance policy (adopted April 2022, card_0016), the social media policy (effective September 2023, card_0019), and the employee handbook extract (last updated August 2021, card_0020) each print \"225 Binney Street, Suite 400, Cambridge, MA 02142\". card_0022's fact inventory independently confirms this, noting \"Legacy policies (2019–2024) all say 225 Binney Street\" in contrast to the 235 Binney Street address in the newer 2024–2025 documents."}, {"id": "c59", "claim": "The Charter, the draft resolution, and the email give the office address as 235 Binney Street.", "type": "other", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0010 shows the draft board resolution stating the Company's principal executive offices are at \"235 Binney Street, Suite 400, Cambridge, MA 02142\". card_0011 shows the Audit Committee Charter's stockholder-request block giving \"235 Binney Street, Suite 400 Cambridge, MA 02142\". card_0006's full text (cards/full/card_0006.txt, lines 281-283) shows the Calloway email listing the office address as \"Greenfield Therapeutics, Inc. / 235 Binney Street, Suite 400 / Cambridge, MA 02142\". All three documents give 235 Binney Street as stated in the claim; the 225 Binney variants appear only in other (older) source documents, not in these three."}, {"id": "c60", "claim": "The memorandum lists the legacy corrections as an outstanding item.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0026 shows the memorandum (/tmp/draft/memo.md) containing \"Section VII. Outstanding Items and Action Items\", whose table row 14 lists \"legacy address corrections (225 → 235 Binney)\" as an outstanding item (owner GC + W&C, target Q2 2025). card_0022 corroborates the underlying discrepancy: legacy policies (2019–2024) all use 225 Binney Street while the new address is 235 Binney, with the instruction to \"flag legacy updates\". The cards therefore show the memorandum listing the legacy corrections as an outstanding item, as claimed."}, {"id": "c61", "claim": "The first pandoc build exited with code 127 because a command called `file`, which is not installed, was invoked.", "type": "runtime_failure", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0029 (turn 35) shows the first pandoc build of the generated deliverables: `pandoc code.md ... -o /app/workspace/code-of-business-conduct-and-ethics.docx && pandoc memo.md ... && ls -la ... && file /app/workspace/...docx`, which returned \"Exit code 127\". The full card (cards/full/card_0029.txt) shows the exact cause: `/bin/bash: line 1: file: command not found`. Both pandoc builds had succeeded (the two new .docx files appear in the ls output with Oct 5 timestamps), and the 127 came from the `file` command at the end of the chain, matching the claim."}, {"id": "c62", "claim": "The agent did not rerun the failed build step.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0029 is the only failing build in the trace: the combined build command exited 127 solely because of \"/bin/bash: line 1: file: command not found\" (the pandoc builds had succeeded; the new .docx files appear in the same output). card_0030/card_0031 show the agent did not rerun that step; it instead substituted a python3 zipfile check to verify the docx packages. No later card invokes `file` or reruns the failed turn-35 command (a grep over all cards shows card_0029 is the only Exit-code-127 / \"file: command not found\" occurrence). The later pandoc invocations in card_0051 (turn 63) and card_0055 (turn 67) are rebuilds after content edits, which card_0064 (the agent's own verification log … [177 more characters]"}, {"id": "c63", "claim": "The agent's zip check passed for both .docx files.", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0031 shows the agent running a python zipfile check over both code-of-business-conduct-and-ethics.docx and drafting-memorandum.docx; the output reports \"valid OOXML docx: True\" for each (16 parts, non-trivial document.xml sizes), so the zip check passed for both .docx files."}, {"id": "c64", "claim": "The agent wrote that \"Both documents render correctly.\"", "type": "other", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0060 (turn 74) shows the assistant message: \"Both documents render correctly. Let me do a final traceability pass on the remaining riskier statements, then close out the process record.\" — the exact sentence the claim says the agent wrote, in the card the report passage cites."}, {"id": "c65", "claim": "The scenario dates are early 2025: the email is dated January 27, the memorandum February 28, and adoption March 18, 2025.", "type": "other", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0006 shows the Calloway instruction email header \"Date: Mon, 27 Jan 2025 04:17:00 -0000\", matching the claimed January 27 email date. card_0026 shows the drafting memorandum's own header block with \"DATE: February 28, 2025\". card_0010 shows the draft board resolution \"Adopted by Unanimous Written Consent March 18, 2025\", matching the claimed March 18, 2025 adoption. All three scenario dates match the claim as stated."}, {"id": "c66", "claim": "Because the March 2025 milestones, the Q2 2025 CCO hire, and the April 2025 policy deadline have all passed, every date in the drafts must be reset before use.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 5, "verdict": "unsupported_or_contradicted", "why": "The claim asserts the report identified unresolved work: that the March 2025 milestones, the Q2 2025 CCO hire, and the April 2025 policy deadline have all passed, so every date in the drafts must be reset before use. The trace shows the opposite. card_0022 (source fact inventory) treats all three as task-internal future commitments on a Jan–Feb 2025 timeline: Board adoption/effective date March 18, 2025 per the draft resolution, CCO hire Q2 2025, and a standalone data-privacy program \"by ~April 2025\" (Thornberry Phase 3). card_0028 shows the agent deliberately setting the Code's cover to \"For adoption by the Board of Directors on March 18, 2025 | Effective March 18, 2025\" and keeping it. car … [552 more characters]"}, {"id": "c67", "claim": "The zip and OOXML structure check passed for both .docx files; each contains the core parts and a non-trivial document body.", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0031 shows a zipfile/OOXML structure check run over both .docx files: each reported \"valid OOXML docx: True\" with 16 parts including [Content_Types].xml and word/document.xml, and non-trivial document bodies (119378 and 92175 bytes). card_0032 confirms the agent treated both as valid."}, {"id": "c68", "claim": "The plain-text conversion of both files succeeded.", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0033 shows the agent running pandoc on both /app/workspace files with -t plain (code-of-business-conduct-and-ethics.docx -> code_check.txt, drafting-memorandum.docx -> memo_check.txt) and receiving successful word counts (8396 and 4653 words), i.e. both plain-text conversions succeeded. card_0055 shows the memo docx rebuilt after final edits being re-converted to plain text successfully (valid OOXML: True, downstream greps ran). card_0064's verification log records the round-trips as clean (code ~8,400 words, memo ~4,700 words) and notes \"final round-trip re-verified.\" The claim that the plain-text conversion of both files succeeded is exactly what these cards show."}, {"id": "c69", "claim": "The agent reports about 8,400 words for the Code and about 4,700 words for the memo.", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0064 (the agent's verification log) states \"code ~8,400 words, memo ~4,700 words\", matching the claimed figures exactly. card_0033 shows the underlying measurement (wc -w on the pandoc plain-text round-trips) giving 8396 words for the Code and 4653 for the memo, so the ~8,400 / ~4,700 report is accurate."}, {"id": "c70", "claim": "The first pass of the required-terms check returned zeros for several terms, including \"National Labor Relations\".", "type": "runtime_failure", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0034 shows the first pass of the required-terms check: a grep -c loop over code_check.txt that returned 0 for several terms, including \"Item 406 of Regulation S-K\", \"pre-approval\", \"full, fair, accurate, timely, and understandable\", \"General Data Protection Regulation\", \"National Labor Relations\", and \"Swiss Federal Act on Data Protection\". The full card text confirms \"0  National Labor Relations\". card_0035's assistant remark (\"Several zeros are likely line-wrap artifacts\") and card_0036's re-check (which then finds \"National Labor Relations\" once) corroborate that these were the first-pass zeros. The claim is stated exactly as the evidence shows."}, {"id": "c71", "claim": "The agent reran the required-terms check on flattened text.", "type": "recovery", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0034 (turn 41) shows the first required-terms sweep over code_check.txt returning zeros for several terms (e.g., \"Item 406 of Regulation S-K\", \"pre-approval\", \"full, fair, accurate, timely, and understandable\"). card_0035 records the agent attributing the zeros to line-wrap artifacts and deciding to \"re-check with normalized whitespace.\" card_0036 (turn 43) shows exactly that: tr '\\n' ' ' flattens code_check.txt (and memo_check.txt) into *_flat.txt files, and the zeroed required terms are re-grepped against code_flat.txt, confirming hits. The claim that the agent reran the required-terms check on flattened text, attributed to card 36, matches the trace."}, {"id": "c72", "claim": "The \"Chang\" hits from the terms check were false positives for \"changes\".", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0038 shows the terms check grepping case-insensitively for \"Chang\" in the Code's flattened text, returning five hits: \"changes to the Board\", \"Securities Exchange Act\", \"circumstances change, and you will\", \"circumstances change; (b) require\", and \"scientific exchange, educational\". None of these is the surname \"Chang\"; every hit is a substring of another word (change/changes, with two also matching \"exchange\"), so the false-positive characterization in the claim holds. card_0039 records the agent's conclusion immediately after: \"The Code is clean (the 'Chang' hits are just the word 'changes').\" Minor wording nuance: not all hits were \"changes\" specifically (two are \"change\" singular an … [227 more characters]"}, {"id": "c73", "claim": "The agent reported that Brenner, Solstice, Henrik Sørensen, Ridgepoint, and the CFO trade do not appear in the Code.", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0037 shows the agent grepping code_flat.txt for the must-not-contain strings and getting 0 hits for Brenner, Solstice, Henrik, Ridgepoint, and for the CFO-trade markers \"4,500\" and \"October 28\" (Sørensen appears only as Annika Sørensen, the named covered person). card_0064 shows the verification log recording the outcome: \"0 hits for: Brenner, Solstice, Henrik, Ridgepoint, ... October 28 trade ... PASS\", confirming the agent reported these names and the CFO trade do not appear in the Code."}, {"id": "c74", "claim": "The Section 12 title matches the draft resolution.", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0010 (draft-board-resolution.md) names \"Section 12 thereof (the \\\"Supplemental Code of Ethics for Senior Financial Officers\\\")\" as the code-of-ethics designation; card_0059 (rendered code, code_check.txt) shows Section 12's heading as \"12. Supplemental Code of Ethics for Senior Financial Officers\". The titles are word-for-word identical, so the report's \"Matches\" conclusion for item 59 is borne out by the evidence."}, {"id": "c75", "claim": "In the hotline wording review, the Code uses \"once established\".", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0061 shows the agent's hotline phrasing check of the Code (§13.2 \"How to Report\"), whose text reads: \"the Company's compliance reporting channels, including the confidential ethics email (ethics@greenfieldtx.com) and, once established, the Company's third-party Compliance Hotline\" — confirming the Code uses \"once established\" for the hotline. card_0065's process notes independently record \"Hotline described with 'once established' hedge (vendor not yet engaged),\" corroborating the claim."}, {"id": "c76", "claim": "In the hotline wording review, the Code omits the reception number.", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0061 shows the hotline wording check of Code §13.2: the code describes the third-party Compliance Hotline with an \"once established\" hedge and states \"The hotline's telephone number and web address will be published on the Company's intranet and in the Whistleblower Policy when the service is activated\" — no reception number appears in the hotline language. card_0065 (process notes) confirms the decision explicitly: \"Hotline described with 'once established' hedge (vendor not yet engaged); reception number 617-555-0142 deliberately excluded from Code; memo recommends removing it from the Whistleblower Policy immediately.\""}, {"id": "c77", "claim": "The checks did not establish that any requirement is legally correct.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The claim is that the verification checks did not establish that any requirement is legally correct. The cards show the full scope of the checks and none reaches legal correctness. card_0064 (the verification log) enumerates every check as: OOXML/round-trip format checks, requirement checks marked PASS purely on presence of required elements and phrases (e.g., \"Nasdaq Listing Rule 5610\", SOX 406 five elements \"verbatim in substance\"), a privacy check (zero hits for private names in the public Code), and fact spot-checks traceable to source documents. card_0034 and card_0036 show the actual mechanism: grep -c / grep -o counts of keywords in the draft text — string-presence tests, not legal an … [253 more characters]"}, {"id": "c78", "claim": "The checks did not establish that the citations match the rules.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The claim is that the performed checks did not establish that the document's citations match the rules. The trace shows every verification step that was run, and none of them validated citation accuracy. card_0034 loops over ~50 citation strings (\"Nasdaq Listing Rule 5610\", \"Section 406\", \"Item 406 of Regulation S-K\", \"Section 806\", etc.) and only counts grep hits in the code text; card_0036 re-greps the flattened text for the initially zero-hit strings; card_0051 rebuilds the docx and re-runs the same presence/absence grep loop (OK/MISS by count). These establish that citation strings occur in the documents, not that the cited rules actually require what the documents say about them. card_0 … [641 more characters]"}, {"id": "c79", "claim": "The checks did not establish that the files open and display correctly in Word or another viewer.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The saved checks are all structural or text-based: card_0031 verifies the two\n.docx files are valid OOXML zip packages (Content_Types + word/document.xml\npresent) via python zipfile, and card_0033/card_0055 round-trip them through\n`pandoc <docx> -t plain` for word counts and content greps; card_0064's\nverification log labels these \"File-format checks (evaluator opens with\npandoc)\" and its only Word reference notes the TOC is a live Word field that\n\"populates on open in Word,\" i.e., a behavior observed in the trace that pandoc\nround-trip does NOT reproduce. A grep over all cards for\nword|viewer|open|display found no instance of the files being opened in Word\nor any viewer. So the checks as ru … [124 more characters]"}, {"id": "c80", "claim": "The checks did not establish that the table of contents generated by the build is populated.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The only checks the trace shows are: the pandoc build with --toc (card_0029), OOXML validity + part count (card_0031), plain-text round-trip (card_0033), and keyword greps over the round-trip text (cards 0034-0061). card_0058 shows the round-trip output begins with a bare \"Table of Contents\" line followed immediately by the CEO-message placeholder — no TOC entries or page numbers appear, so the checks cannot show the TOC is populated. card_0064's verification log itself states, under \"Known non-issues\", that \"Code TOC is a live Word field (populates on open in Word); pandoc plain-text round-trip shows the 'Table of Contents' caption only.\" No card inspects document.xml TOC entries or otherwi … [106 more characters]"}, {"id": "c81", "claim": "The checks did not establish that every statement of fact matches its source.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The saved trace shows the verification \"checks\" were finite, curated-string grep sweeps, not exhaustive fact-to-source matching. card_0034 greps the Code for ~50 hand-picked \"required elements\" strings; card_0040 does the same for the Memo with a fixed list of ~80 fact strings (reporting 0/1 hit counts per string). card_0064's verification log records \"Fact spot-checks traceable to sources\" as PASS — i.e., the agent itself characterized the fact check as a spot-check of a sampled list of figures/dates, with some strings deliberately absent (e.g., \"Wei-Lin Chang\" fixed later by an edit per card_0040's zero hit). Nothing in the checks establishes that every statement of fact in the deliverable … [101 more characters]"}, {"id": "c82", "claim": "The agent made spot-checks against the source documents, covering the email's lack of a facilitation reference, the \"Section 5.3\" source, policy §4, the Charter and resolution wording, the Thornberry hotline details, and the memorandum's outstanding list.", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0064 (the verification-log write) records each spot-check the claim names: \"facilitation §5.3-vs-§4 citation\" under source discrepancies; \"617-555-0142 = reception\" under \"Fact spot-checks traceable to sources\" (the Thornberry hotline details); \"Section 12 ... exact title matching Adoption Resolution designation\" plus \"consistent with Audit Committee Charter §V.D.2/§VI\" (the Charter and resolution wording); and \"Outstanding items: 15-row action table with owners/targets (memo §VII) ... PASS\" (the memorandum's outstanding list). The checks ran against the source documents themselves: card_0006 shows the Calloway instructions email read (it contains no facilitation reference, matching the … [259 more characters]"}, {"id": "c83", "claim": "Most authorities in the drafts were not checked against any text in the record.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The record (all workspace source documents, fully inventoried in card_0022) contained only named\nreferences/paraphrases of the legal authorities (Nasdaq 5610, SOX 406/Item 406, UK Bribery Act,\nPhRMA/EFPIA, etc.), never the authorities' text. Every verification step in the trace is a\nstring-presence check on the drafts' own round-trip text, not a cross-check against any record\ntext: card_0034 greps code_check.txt for ~60 authority/element strings (verifying the draft\nmentions them, several returning 0 and never re-checked against a source), card_0040 does the\nsame for memo_flat.txt, and card_0064's verification log marks all requirement checks PASS\nagainst the agent's derived list with no rec … [421 more characters]"}, {"id": "c84", "claim": "The unchecked authorities include Nasdaq Rule 5610, SOX 406 and Item 406, the Item 404 $120,000 threshold, the four-business-day Form 8-K Item 5.05 deadline, SOX 301 and Rule 10A-3, SOX 806, Dodd-Frank 21F, the FCPA facilitation exception, the UK Bribery Act 2010, Swiss Criminal Code art. 322ter, the revDSG, GDPR, HIPAA, 21 CFR Part 11, the PhRMA and EFPIA codes, Rule 10b5-1, and Section 16.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 5, "verdict": "unsupported_or_contradicted", "why": "The claim states that the enumerated authorities (Nasdaq Rule 5610, SOX 406/Item 406, the Item 404 $120,000 threshold, the four-business-day Form 8-K Item 5.05 deadline, SOX 301/Rule 10A-3, SOX 806, Dodd-Frank 21F, the FCPA facilitation exception, UK Bribery Act 2010, Swiss CC art. 322ter, revDSG, GDPR, HIPAA, 21 CFR Part 11, PhRMA/EFPIA codes, Rule 10b5-1, and Section 16) were \"unchecked.\" The saved trace shows the exact opposite: card_0064 (the verification log) records a PASS line for each of these specific items — \"Nasdaq Listing Rule 5610 ... PASS,\" \"SOX 406 / Item 406 Reg S-K: five elements verbatim in substance ... PASS,\" \"≥$120,000 Item 404 → Audit Committee pre-approval ... PASS,\" \" … [712 more characters]"}, {"id": "c85", "claim": "The agent's fact inventory (card 22) shows these authorities came from its own knowledge.", "type": "other", "section": 5, "verdict": "unsupported_or_contradicted", "why": "Card 22 is the \"Source fact inventory & discrepancies\" write (source-facts.md), whose \"Key legal parameters\" section lists the authorities in question: Nasdaq 5610, SOX 406/Item 406, Item 404, 8-K Item 5.05, SOX 301/10A-3, SOX 806, Dodd-Frank 21F, FCPA 15 USC 78dd-1, UK Bribery Act 2010, Swiss CC Art. 322ter, revDSG, GDPR, FDA 21 CFR Part 11, HIPAA, PhRMA/EFPIA Codes, 10b5-1, Rule 10b-5. The claim says card 22 shows these came from the agent's own knowledge. The trace shows the opposite: the same authorities appear verbatim in the workspace source documents the agent read in the immediately preceding turns (cards 0006–0021). Card 0006 (Calloway drafting-instructions email) supplies Nasdaq 56 … [525 more characters]"}, {"id": "c86", "claim": "The user is directed to have qualified counsel review the Code and the memorandum, and check every regulatory citation against the primary text.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 5, "verdict": "unsupported_or_contradicted", "why": "The claim says the user directed that qualified counsel review the Code and the memorandum and check every regulatory citation against the primary text. The saved instructions from the user (Calloway's email, card_0006) contain no such directive: the user himself (General Counsel) is the reviewer — the timeline has Calloway reviewing the draft by the week of March 3 and circulating it to Board members (Whitfield, Volkova) — while the drafting is done by outside counsel Lindström/Okafor of Whitfield & Crane LLP, who are already counsel. The closest actual instruction is to make the SOX 406 language \"track the statutory and regulatory requirements closely enough that we can make that represent … [548 more characters]"}, {"id": "c87", "claim": "The user is directed to correct the \"Section 5.3\" attribution in the memorandum before it circulates, and in the fact inventory.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 5, "verdict": "unsupported_or_contradicted", "why": "The only \"Section 5.3\" citation issue in the trace is the one found in card_0022 (fact inventory): Calloway's email cites the facilitation-payments exception as \"Section 5.3\" of the Anti-Corruption Policy while the actual policy section is §4, with the resolution already decided by the agent (\"cite §4 in memo\"). The trace shows this item was handled, not left to the user: card_0064's verification log records \"Source discrepancies documented (memo §VI): ... facilitation §5.3-vs-§4 citation ... PASS\", and card_0066 (the agent's final message to the user) describes the memo's source-discrepancy log — including \"the §5.3/§4 citation\" — as completed, verified work, with no outstanding instruction … [430 more characters]"}, {"id": "c88", "claim": "The user is directed to open both .docx files in Word or another viewer and check layout, the table of contents, and the appendix.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 5, "verdict": "unsupported_or_contradicted", "why": "The claim is that the user is directed to open both .docx files in Word or another viewer and check layout, the table of contents, and the appendix. The saved trace shows the complete final assistant message (card_0066): it reports both deliverables as \"complete and verified,\" describes their contents and the automated verification (OOXML package check, pandoc round-trip, citation/threshold checks, privacy check), and lists no outstanding user action. A grep over all 66 cards finds no \"open both\", \"in Word\", \"viewer\", \"check layout\", or \"table of contents\" instruction anywhere. The only Word/TOC references are card_0064's verification-log note that the Code's TOC is a live Word field that po … [256 more characters]"}, {"id": "c89", "claim": "The user is directed to check the draft resolution's waiver language against the Charter, confirm whether a Board delegation to the Audit Committee is needed, and finalize the resolution.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 5, "verdict": "unsupported_or_contradicted", "why": "The trace shows the memo's actual open items to the user in detail, and the claimed\nthree-part instruction does not match them. Card_0026 (full memo) §VIII \"Confirmations\nRequested\" item 1 asks the user only to concur with the Audit Committee (as delegated\nby the Board) waiver-authority recommendation — not to \"check the draft resolution's\nwaiver language against the Charter,\" because the agent already did that check itself:\nmemo §V.C states the Audit Committee Charter §V.D.2 \"already provides\" the waiver\nauthority, \"the Code and Charter are thus consistent without amending the Charter,\"\nand \"the Adoption Resolution already tracks this formulation\"; card_0010 shows the\ndraft resolution itsel … [890 more characters]"}, {"id": "c90", "claim": "The user is directed to name the controller and confirm the covered persons under Section 12.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 5, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0026 shows the delivered drafting memo containing \"VIII. Confirmations Requested Before the Week of March 3\", item 2: \"Controller — name and exact title of the current principal accounting officer/controller, to finalize Code § 12.1\" — i.e., the client (Calloway) is directed to name the controller. card_0059 shows Code § 12.1 \"Covered Persons\" listing the CEO, CFO, and \"the Company's controller\" by title only, so naming the controller finalizes/confirms Section 12's covered persons, matching the report's phrasing. card_0064's verification log corroborates: \"controller by title (name pending per memo § IV ask)\"."}, {"id": "c91", "claim": "The user is directed to assign owners and dates to the 15 outstanding items, starting with the hotline vendor and the Brenner review, which the memorandum says must precede rollout.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 5, "verdict": "unsupported_or_contradicted", "why": "card_0026 preserves the memorandum the claim quotes. Its §VII \"Outstanding Items\" opens \"The following remain open, with suggested owners and timing (for your presentation to the Audit Committee and the Board)\" and then gives a 15-row table that already assigns a suggested owner and target to every item — so the memorandum does not direct the user to assign owners and dates; it has already done so. On ordering/rollout, the table targets: item 1 (remove placeholder reception number) \"Immediate\"; item 2 \"Engage third-party hotline vendor … publish number/URL in Whistleblower Policy and Code rollout materials\" targeted \"FY2025 (Q2)\"; item 3 \"Complete remedial review of the Brenner incident\" tar … [570 more characters]"}, {"id": "c92", "claim": "The user is directed to decide how the Audit Committee will handle the CFO share purchase, the Sørensen consulting arrangement, and the Solstice contract, as the memorandum recommends.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 5, "verdict": "unsupported_or_contradicted", "why": "The claim bundles three matters as open items the user must \"decide how the Audit Committee will handle ... as the memorandum recommends.\" The memorandum in the trace treats them differently.\n\n(1) CFO share purchase: the memo does not direct the Audit Committee to handle it at all. Memo §V.B (card_0026) states the Oct 28, 2024 purchase of 5,000 GRNT shares by CFO Rachel Dominguez was reviewed retrospectively, no MNPI evidence was identified, it is not being treated as a violation, and the \"Recommended file actions\" are documentation (record the limited retrospective review in the compliance file, confirm timely Section 16 Form 4, no further disclosure). The card_0064 verification log likewis … [1,433 more characters]"}, {"id": "c93", "claim": "The figure depicts the agent's work as eight numbered groups arranged in two rows of four, linked by left-to-right arrows (1→2→3→4 in the top row, 5→6→7→8 in the bottom row; no arrow is drawn between group 4 and group 5), with the groups' card ranges spanning cards 1–66 contiguously.", "type": "other", "section": null, "verdict": "unverifiable", "why": "The claim is about how sample_report.html#figure-1.svg depicts the agent's work (eight numbered groups in two rows of four, arrows 1→2→3→4 and 5→6→7→8 with none 4→5, group annotations spanning cards 1–66 contiguously). That figure is a report artifact and is not among the saved evidence: the visible files are only input/claim.md, input/cards/index.md, and the 66 card files, and no sample_report.html or figure-1.svg exists anywhere in the permitted tree (checked via find). I read the card index and grepped all 66 cards for figure/diagram/arrow/row/card-range content; the cards contain only the task trajectory (extracting sources, drafting the code and memo, verification passes) and never refe … [559 more characters]"}, {"id": "c94", "claim": "The figure depicts the first group, \"1 Inventory and notes\" (cards 1–5), as listing inputs and extracting sources.", "type": "other", "section": null, "verdict": "supported", "why": "Cards 1–5 do exactly what the figure's label \"listing inputs and extracting sources\" describes for the first group \"1 Inventory and notes\": card_0001 lists the workspace input files (the policy .docx sources and the instructions .eml); card_0002 inventories the process directory and confirms pandoc/python3; card_0003 extracts all sources by converting every .docx to markdown under /tmp/extract/; cards 0004–0005 record that setup in process notes, matching the \"and notes\" part of the group name. The figure's depiction of this card range is therefore faithful to the saved evidence."}, {"id": "c95", "claim": "The figure shows the work moving to group \"2 Source reading\" (cards 6–21), in which an email, Thornberry, and policies were read.", "type": "task_transition", "section": null, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The cards in the claimed range (6–21) are precisely a contiguous source-reading group. card_0006 reads the Calloway drafting-instructions email; card_0008 reads the Thornberry Compliance readiness assessment; card_0010 reads the draft board resolution; card_0011 the audit-committee charter; and card_0013, card_0014, card_0016, card_0017, card_0019, card_0020 read the conflict-of-interest, insider-trading, anti-corruption, whistleblower, social-media policies and the employee-handbook ethics extract. card_0021 closes the group with \"I've now read all ten source documents,\" matching the ten Read commands in cards 6–21. This is consistent with the figure depicting this segment as group \"2 Sourc … [55 more characters]"}, {"id": "c96", "claim": "The figure shows the work moving to group \"3 Fact inventory\" (card 22), which holds source facts and discrepancies.", "type": "task_transition", "section": null, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The claim describes the figure's third of eight work groups, anchored at card 22, as a \"Fact inventory\" holding source facts and discrepancies. The index confirms the report's \"eight groups by card range\" correspond to the 8 chunks, and card_0022 falls in chunk/group 3 (which spans cards 6-32). card_0022 is a Write of /app/workspace/process/source-facts.md titled \"Source fact inventory & discrepancies\", whose content is exactly source facts (company, people, board, legal parameters, Thornberry findings) plus a dedicated \"Source discrepancies found\" section. card_0021 (turn 26) shows the transition: the agent states it has finished reading all ten sources and will \"record the key facts and di … [237 more characters]"}, {"id": "c97", "claim": "The figure shows the work moving to group \"4 Code and memo\" (cards 23–28), covering the code, the memo, and a date edit.", "type": "task_transition", "section": null, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The cards in the claimed range do hold the code, the memo, and a date edit as stated.\ncard_0023 is the assistant announcing it will draft the Code; card_0024 is the Write of the\nfull Code to /tmp/draft/code.md; card_0026 is the Write of the drafting memorandum to\n/tmp/draft/memo.md; and card_0028 is an Edit to code.md changing the date line from\n\"Adopted by the Board of Directors | Effective March 18, 2025\" to \"For adoption by the\nBoard of Directors on March 18, 2025 | Effective March 18, 2025\", which is the date edit.\nThe intervening cards (0025, 0027) are brief observations/commentary, consistent with the\nreport's note that not all cards in a range are substantive. The figure file itself i … [90 more characters]"}, {"id": "c98", "claim": "The figure shows the work moving to group \"5 Build and checks\" (cards 29–43), covering a build, zip and term checks.", "type": "task_transition", "section": null, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The figure's claim that the work transitions into a \"build and checks\" phase over cards 29-43 covering a build, a zip check, and term checks is borne out by the cards. card_0028 is the last drafting Edit to code.md, and card_0029 begins the build (pandoc markdown->docx for both the Code and the memo), marking the transition. card_0031 is the zip/OOXML check (python zipfile asserting 'valid OOXML docx' with [Content_Types].xml and word/document.xml). cards 0033-0043 are the term checks: pandoc round-trip to plain (card_0033), grep for required CODE elements (card_0034), flat-term counts (card_0036), \"must NOT contain\" private-matter terms (card_0037), MEMO required facts (card_0040), memo ter … [317 more characters]"}, {"id": "c99", "claim": "The figure shows the work moving to group \"6 Memo edits\" (cards 44–53), which comprised five edits and a name fix.", "type": "task_transition", "section": null, "verdict": "supported", "why": "Cards 44-53 are exactly the memo-edit phase: card_0044 announces \"I'll make five precision edits to the memo: IPO background, full names for Chang/Brenner, and the committee chairs in the waiver discussion.\" The range then holds Edit calls to /tmp/draft/memo.md at card_0045 (IPO background), card_0046 (full name for Chang), card_0048 (committee chairs in the waiver discussion), card_0049 (Brenner's full name), a rebuild at card_0051, then card_0052 (\"the memo never spells out the CEO's full name. Quick fix\") and card_0053, the name fix spelling out Dr. Annika Sørensen as CEO. That is five edits in the group, the last one the name fix, matching the claim's composition."}, {"id": "c100", "claim": "The figure shows the work moving to group \"7 Rebuild and checks\" (cards 54–63), in which both files were rebuilt and more checks were run.", "type": "task_transition", "section": null, "verdict": "unsupported_or_contradicted", "why": "The cards in the claimed group (54–63) show the final memo rebuild plus a\nstring of content/rendering checks: card_0054's step says \"Now rebuild the\nmemo docx with the final edit, and run a final end-to-end check,\"\ncard_0055 rebuilds only drafting-memorandum.docx and greps its round-trip\noutput, and cards 56–61 run further grep/sed/head checks on the flat files.\nBut \"both files were rebuilt\" in this group is wrong: the only pandoc rebuild\nof code-of-business-conduct-and-ethics.docx in the trajectory is card_0051\n(turn 63), which predates the claimed range, and no card in 54–63 issues a\npandoc build of the code file. card_0063's directory listing confirms the\nfinal code docx was written at 04 … [222 more characters]"}, {"id": "c101", "claim": "The figure shows the work moving to group \"8 Logs and close-out\" (cards 64–66), covering a verification log and a final message.", "type": "task_transition", "section": null, "verdict": "supported", "why": "Card 64 is a Write of \"# Verification log (task completion check)\" documenting deliverables, file-format checks, and requirement checks — the verification log the claim names. Card 65 is a Write updating process/notes.md to \"Status: COMPLETE (verified)\", closing out the process record. Card 66 is an empty-command final assistant message summarizing both deliverables as complete and verified — the final message. Together cards 64–66 are the last three cards of the trajectory (100% position), matching the claim that the final group covers a verification log and a final message."}, {"id": "c102", "claim": "The figure states the final state of the task: a verification log (cards 64–66) and a final message were produced, and group 8 is labelled \"Logs and close-out\", with no remaining work shown after it.", "type": "outcome_claim", "section": null, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The trace's tail matches the claim exactly. card_0064 (turn 79) is a Write of \"# Verification log (task completion check)\" documenting the deliverables, format checks, and all PASS requirement checks — the verification log the claim names. card_0065 (turn 81) updates the process notes with \"Status: COMPLETE (verified)\" — close-out. card_0066 (turn 82) has an empty command and is the final assistant message summarizing \"Both deliverables are complete and verified. Final state: ...\". The index confirms card_0066 is the last of 66 cards (trajectory 100%, turn 82 of 82), so no work is shown after it; all three close-out cards sit in chunk 8, the final group. The figure's \"Logs and close-out\" lab … [59 more characters]"}], "recoveries": [{"event": "event_010", "verdict": "adapted", "why": "card_0029 is the failing command chain: the pandoc conversions and ls succeeded, but the trailing `file ...docx` failed with \"file: command not found\" (exit 127), leaving the deliverable binaries unverified. The agent's next attempt at that same verification goal, card_0031, replaced the missing `file` command with a python3 zipfile check that opens each .docx and confirms the OOXML parts ([Content_Types].xml, word/document.xml) exist and are non-trivial — a different command/approach addressing the exact failure (missing `file` binary) — and it passes (both \"valid OOXML docx: True\")."}]}, "branches": [], "reports": {"html": "<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title>Report on the agent's Code of Business Conduct drafting session (Greenfield Therapeutics)<\/title>\n<style>\nbody{font-family:Georgia,\"Times New Roman\",serif;line-height:1.55;color:#1d1d1f;max-width:980px;margin:0 auto;padding:24px 20px 60px;background:#fff}\nh1{font-size:1.7em;margin-bottom:.2em}\nh2{font-size:1.3em;border-bottom:1px solid #ccc;padding-bottom:.2em;margin-top:2em}\n.meta{color:#555;font-size:.92em}\ntable{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;margin:1em 0;font-size:.92em}\nth,td{border:1px solid #bbb;padding:6px 8px;vertical-align:top;text-align:left}\nth{background:#f0f0f0}\n.callout{border-left:4px solid #b35c00;background:#fff7ec;padding:10px 14px;margin:1em 0}\nfigure{margin:1.5em 0}\nfigure svg{width:100%;height:auto;display:block}\nfigcaption{font-size:.9em;color:#444;margin-top:.4em}\ncode{font-family:Menlo,Consolas,monospace;font-size:.9em}\n<\/style>\n<\/head>\n<body>\n\n<header>\n<h1>Report on the agent's Code of Business Conduct drafting session<\/h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Client: Greenfield Therapeutics, Inc. (Nasdaq: GRNT). Scenario dates: January to March 2025. This report is based only on the session record in <code>cards/<\/code>: <code>cards/index.md<\/code> and <code>card_0001<\/code> through <code>card_0066<\/code>, with <code>cards/full/<\/code> used where a card was truncated. Quotations were checked against the full evidence where noted.<\/p>\n<\/header>\n\n<main>\n\n<section id=\"summary\">\n<h2>1. Summary<\/h2>\n<p>The agent produced two Word documents in <code>/app/workspace/<\/code>: a Code of Business Conduct and Ethics (about 8,400 words) and a privileged drafting memorandum (about 4,700 words). The memorandum is written as from a named partner at Whitfield &amp; Crane LLP to the company's General Counsel. Both files are valid Word packages, and the agent's string checks found the required terms. No lawyer reviewed either document, no citation was checked against statute or rule text, and neither was viewed in a rendered form.<\/p>\n<div class=\"callout\">\n<p><strong>Key points<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>The Code is a working draft for counsel review. It is not ready for adoption.<\/li>\n<li>The draft board resolution was not edited or finalized. The memorandum says it needs no changes, and the record does not support that conclusion.<\/li>\n<li>The memorandum attributes a \"Section 5.3\" reference to the client's instructions. The reference comes from the Thornberry report. The client's email does not contain it.<\/li>\n<li>Most regulatory citations come from the agent's own knowledge, not from any document in the record.<\/li>\n<li>The memorandum's outstanding-items table lists 15 actions. The record shows none of them as completed.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/div>\n<\/section>\n\n<section id=\"request\">\n<h2>2. What the agent was asked to do<\/h2>\n<p>The client's request is the email from James Calloway, General Counsel and Corporate Secretary, to Sarah Lindström of Whitfield &amp; Crane LLP, dated January 27, 2025 (card 6). It asks for:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>A single Code satisfying Nasdaq Listing Rule 5610 and SOX 406 (Item 406 of Regulation S-K). It should be organized by topic, include a CEO letter placeholder, apply globally with a local-law savings clause, and be posted publicly and filed as Exhibit 14 to the FY2024 Form 10-K.<\/li>\n<li>Tiered related-party transaction rules. The existing $5,000 internal disclosure threshold stays, and transactions of $120,000 or more (Item 404) escalate to the Audit Committee.<\/li>\n<li>Insider-trading rules covering pre-clearance, blackout periods, and Rule 10b5-1 plans.<\/li>\n<li>A waiver procedure with Board or committee approval and disclosure within four business days (Form 8-K Item 5.05).<\/li>\n<li>Responses to all 14 findings in the Thornberry Compliance Readiness Assessment (June 28, 2024).<\/li>\n<li>Designation of Calloway as interim compliance officer, with a Chief Compliance Officer hire planned for Q2 2025.<\/li>\n<li>A companion drafting memorandum, and finalization of the draft board resolution \"once the Code is in shape.\"<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The email's timeline: draft by February 28, 2025; board pre-meeting March 10, 2025; adoption March 18, 2025. The FY2025 compliance budget is $850,000.<\/p>\n<\/section>\n\n<section id=\"sequence\">\n<h2>3. What the agent did, in order<\/h2>\n<figure data-pil-kind=\"diagram\" data-pil-caption=\"The agent's work in eight groups, by card range. Cards in a range are not all substantive; some hold commentary only.\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 940 220\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Eight groups of the agent's work in two rows of four, each labelled with its card range\">\n<defs><marker id=\"arr\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"4\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z\" fill=\"#555\"/><\/marker><\/defs>\n<g font-family=\"Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#1d1d1f\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"10\" width=\"200\" height=\"84\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#eef3f8\" stroke=\"#557\"/>\n<text x=\"20\" y=\"32\" font-weight=\"bold\">1  Inventory and notes<\/text>\n<text x=\"20\" y=\"52\">Cards 1–5<\/text>\n<text x=\"20\" y=\"72\" font-size=\"11\">Inputs listed, sources extracted<\/text>\n<rect x=\"250\" y=\"10\" width=\"200\" height=\"84\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#eef3f8\" stroke=\"#557\"/>\n<text x=\"260\" y=\"32\" font-weight=\"bold\">2  Source reading<\/text>\n<text x=\"260\" y=\"52\">Cards 6–21<\/text>\n<text x=\"260\" y=\"72\" font-size=\"11\">Email, Thornberry, policies read<\/text>\n<rect x=\"490\" y=\"10\" width=\"200\" height=\"84\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#eef3f8\" stroke=\"#557\"/>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"32\" font-weight=\"bold\">3  Fact inventory<\/text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"52\">Card 22<\/text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"72\" font-size=\"11\">Source facts and discrepancies<\/text>\n<rect x=\"730\" y=\"10\" width=\"200\" height=\"84\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#eef3f8\" stroke=\"#557\"/>\n<text x=\"740\" y=\"32\" font-weight=\"bold\">4  Code and memo<\/text>\n<text x=\"740\" y=\"52\">Cards 23–28<\/text>\n<text x=\"740\" y=\"72\" font-size=\"11\">Code, memo, date edit<\/text>\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"120\" width=\"200\" height=\"84\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#f6f6f6\" stroke=\"#777\"/>\n<text x=\"20\" y=\"142\" font-weight=\"bold\">5  Build and checks<\/text>\n<text x=\"20\" y=\"162\">Cards 29–43<\/text>\n<text x=\"20\" y=\"182\" font-size=\"11\">Build, zip and term checks<\/text>\n<rect x=\"250\" y=\"120\" width=\"200\" height=\"84\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#f6f6f6\" stroke=\"#777\"/>\n<text x=\"260\" y=\"142\" font-weight=\"bold\">6  Memo edits<\/text>\n<text x=\"260\" y=\"162\">Cards 44–53<\/text>\n<text x=\"260\" y=\"182\" font-size=\"11\">Five edits and a name fix<\/text>\n<rect x=\"490\" y=\"120\" width=\"200\" height=\"84\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#f6f6f6\" stroke=\"#777\"/>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"142\" font-weight=\"bold\">7  Rebuild and checks<\/text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"162\">Cards 54–63<\/text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"182\" font-size=\"11\">Rebuilt both files; more checks<\/text>\n<rect x=\"730\" y=\"120\" width=\"200\" height=\"84\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#f6f6f6\" stroke=\"#777\"/>\n<text x=\"740\" y=\"142\" font-weight=\"bold\">8  Logs and close-out<\/text>\n<text x=\"740\" y=\"162\">Cards 64–66<\/text>\n<text x=\"740\" y=\"182\" font-size=\"11\">Verification log; final message<\/text>\n<line x1=\"212\" y1=\"52\" x2=\"248\" y2=\"52\" stroke=\"#555\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arr)\"/>\n<line x1=\"452\" y1=\"52\" x2=\"488\" y2=\"52\" stroke=\"#555\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arr)\"/>\n<line x1=\"692\" y1=\"52\" x2=\"728\" y2=\"52\" stroke=\"#555\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arr)\"/>\n<line x1=\"212\" y1=\"162\" x2=\"248\" y2=\"162\" stroke=\"#555\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arr)\"/>\n<line x1=\"452\" y1=\"162\" x2=\"488\" y2=\"162\" stroke=\"#555\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arr)\"/>\n<line x1=\"692\" y1=\"162\" x2=\"728\" y2=\"162\" stroke=\"#555\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arr)\"/>\n<\/g>\n<\/svg>\n<figcaption>Figure: the agent's work in eight groups, by card range. Groups 1 to 4 run left to right in the top row, then groups 5 to 8 in the bottom row.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Group<\/th><th>Cards<\/th><th>What happened<\/th><\/tr><\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>1. Inventory and notes<\/td><td>1–5<\/td><td>Listed the workspace, checked pandoc 2.17.1.1, extracted the source .docx policies to markdown in <code>/tmp/extract<\/code>, and wrote process notes (card 5).<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>2. Source reading<\/td><td>6–21<\/td><td>Read the instruction email (6), the Thornberry assessment (8), the draft board resolution (10), the Audit Committee Charter (11), and six policy documents (13, 14, 16, 17, 19, 20). The Thornberry assessment covers April 15 to June 21, 2024.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>3. Fact inventory<\/td><td>22<\/td><td>Wrote <code>process/source-facts.md<\/code>, covering people, figures, the 14 findings, and source discrepancies. The note contains errors (section 6).<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>4. Code and memo drafts<\/td><td>23–28<\/td><td>Drafted the Code in markdown (<code>/tmp/draft/code.md<\/code>, 15 numbered sections plus an appendix) and the memorandum (<code>/tmp/draft/memo.md<\/code>, card 26). Changed the Code's date line to adoption on March 18, 2025 (card 28).<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>5. First build and checks<\/td><td>29–43<\/td><td>Built both .docx files with pandoc (card 29). The build chain exited with code 127 because a command called <code>file<\/code>, which is not installed, but both files were written. Checked the zip structure (card 31) and converted both files to plain text (card 33). The first term checks returned zeros for several required terms (card 34). The agent reran them on flattened text (card 36) and reported that private matters were absent from the Code (card 37).<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>6. Memo edits<\/td><td>44–53<\/td><td>Edited the memorandum's opening, the Ridgepoint and Solstice facts, the waiver question, the Brenner remediation status, and the biostatistics retainer figure. Card 52 found the CEO's full name missing; card 53 corrected it.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>7. Rebuild and checks<\/td><td>54–63<\/td><td>Rebuilt both files after the edits (cards 51, 55). Checked the CEO name and Code sections (cards 56–59), reviewed the hotline wording (card 61), and copied source files into <code>/app/workspace/process/<\/code> (card 63).<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>8. Logs and close-out<\/td><td>64–66<\/td><td>Wrote a verification log (card 64) and process notes (card 65). The final message (card 66) calls both deliverables \"complete and verified.\"<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<\/section>\n\n<section id=\"deliverables\">\n<h2>4. Deliverables<\/h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>File<\/th><th>Size in record<\/th><th>Contents<\/th><th>Status<\/th><\/tr><\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>code-of-business-conduct-and-ethics.docx<\/code><\/td>\n<td>33,730 bytes (card 29 and card 64)<\/td>\n<td>About 8,400 words. A CEO letter placeholder; Section 1 on scope and applicability; Section 12, the \"Supplemental Code of Ethics for Senior Financial Officers,\" which matches the draft resolution's designation; covered persons Dr. Annika Sørensen (CEO) and Rachel Dominguez (CFO), with the controller covered by title; Appendix A, an acknowledgment form.<\/td>\n<td>Working draft. Not reviewed by counsel. The hotline is described as \"once established.\" The controller is not named.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>drafting-memorandum.docx<\/code><\/td>\n<td>25,390 bytes at first build (card 29); 25,710 bytes in the verification log (card 64)<\/td>\n<td>About 4,700 words. Marked privileged. Sections I to IX, a finding-by-finding matrix for the 14 Thornberry items, resolutions of the client's specific questions, a source-discrepancy list, and an outstanding-items table of 15 rows.<\/td>\n<td>Working draft. Contains the \"Section 5.3\" misattribution (section 6). Dated February 28, 2025, under a named partner's name, with no record of partner review.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Process files in <code>/app/workspace/process/<\/code><\/td>\n<td>Not sized in record<\/td>\n<td>Notes, <code>source-facts.md<\/code>, <code>verification-log.md<\/code>, copies of the markdown sources, and plain-text conversions of both documents.<\/td>\n<td>The verification log's \"PASS\" entries mark term presence, not legal correctness.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<\/section>\n\n<section id=\"thornberry\">\n<h2>5. Thornberry findings and where they stand<\/h2>\n<p>The \"draft treatment\" column is the agent's own mapping (card 22 and card 64). It describes what the text says. It does not establish that a finding is resolved in the view of the company or a regulator. The \"still open\" column is taken from the memorandum's outstanding-items table.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>#<\/th><th>Rating<\/th><th>Finding<\/th><th>Draft treatment<\/th><th>Still open per memorandum<\/th><\/tr><\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>1<\/td><td>Critical<\/td><td>No unified code (Nasdaq 5610)<\/td><td>The Code itself, applying to all directors, officers, and employees<\/td><td>Distribution and acknowledgments from about 410 personnel; CEO letter; website and Exhibit 14 posting<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>2<\/td><td>Critical<\/td><td>Gap in HCP rules; Brenner incident (luxury watch, about €4,500, July 14, 2024)<\/td><td>HCP rules in Code §7; gifts in §8<\/td><td>Brenner remedial review before rollout; Basel training<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>3<\/td><td>Critical<\/td><td>Hotline not operational; Cambridge reception number given instead<\/td><td>Code §13.2 describes a 24/7 multilingual hotline \"once established,\" with no number<\/td><td>Hotline vendor ($85,000); remove 617-555-0142 from the Whistleblower Policy<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>4<\/td><td>Critical<\/td><td>Compliance officer role vacant<\/td><td>Calloway as interim compliance officer (§2.2); CCO structure (§2.3)<\/td><td>CCO search, $275,000 to $325,000, Q2 2025<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>5<\/td><td>High<\/td><td>Conflict-of-interest policy outdated (2019)<\/td><td>Conflicts rules in Code §4<\/td><td>Initial related-party inventory and D&amp;O questionnaires on adoption<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>6<\/td><td>High<\/td><td>Facilitation-payments exception in the 2022 policy<\/td><td>Prohibited in Code §6.2, with a narrow duress exception only<\/td><td>Memo cross-reference misattributed (section 6)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>7<\/td><td>High<\/td><td>No data privacy program<\/td><td>Principles in Code §10<\/td><td>Standalone Data Privacy Policy by about April 2025<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>8<\/td><td>High<\/td><td>No related-party procedures<\/td><td>Code §5: Audit Committee pre-approval at $120,000 or more; recusal; inventory<\/td><td>Audit Committee review of the Sørensen consulting arrangement; ratification of the Solstice contract with Castellano and Chang recused<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>9<\/td><td>High<\/td><td>2021 handbook has no public-company obligations<\/td><td>Superseded under Code §1.3<\/td><td>Conforming amendments to the Whistleblower and Social Media policies<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>10<\/td><td>High<\/td><td>Anti-corruption training never conducted<\/td><td>Training provisions in Code §2.5<\/td><td>Training program, $120,000<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>11<\/td><td>Medium<\/td><td>$5,000 conflict-of-interest threshold misaligned<\/td><td>$5,000 internal disclosure kept; $120,000 and above escalates<\/td><td>None listed separately<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>12<\/td><td>Medium<\/td><td>Whistleblower policy lacks regulatory specificity<\/td><td>Code §13.4 (SOX 806, Dodd-Frank 21F, SEC program)<\/td><td>Whistleblower Policy amendments<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>13<\/td><td>Medium<\/td><td>No gift limits for non-government interactions<\/td><td>Code §8, with a $100 or CHF 100 per-instance threshold<\/td><td>None listed separately<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>14<\/td><td>Medium<\/td><td>No comprehensive training program<\/td><td>Code §2.5<\/td><td>Training program, $120,000<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<\/section>\n\n<section id=\"errors\">\n<h2>6. Errors, discrepancies, and open items<\/h2>\n\n<h3>6.1 Errors in the record<\/h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Misattributed \"Section 5.3.\"<\/strong> The memorandum's facilitation-payments cross-reference says the client's instructions cite the exception as \"Section 5.3\" of the Anti-Corruption Policy. The January 27 email has no facilitation reference (card 6). The \"Section 5.3\" wording appears in the Thornberry report, Finding 6 (card 8). The underlying point is correct: the April 3, 2022 policy places the exception in §4, which allows $200 per payment and $500 per agency per year (card 16). The fact inventory (card 22) repeats the misattribution, and nothing in the record corrects it.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Brenner's title.<\/strong> The fact inventory (card 22) calls Lars Brenner a \"CRO.\" Thornberry (card 8) says Clinical Research Associate, and the memorandum was corrected to that title (card 49).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Office address.<\/strong> The legacy policies give 225 Binney Street. The Charter, the draft resolution, and the email give 235 Binney Street (cards 6, 10, 11). The new drafts use 235. The memorandum lists the legacy corrections as an outstanding item.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Failed build step.<\/strong> The first pandoc build exited with code 127 because a command called <code>file<\/code>, which is not installed (card 29). Both .docx files were written. The agent did not rerun that step; its zip check (card 31) passed for both.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Overstated status.<\/strong> The agent wrote \"Both documents render correctly\" (card 60) and \"complete and verified\" (card 66). Its only rendering evidence is a plain-text conversion.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n\n<h3>6.2 Open items and decisions<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Draft board resolution.<\/strong> The memorandum says the resolution will be finalized with \"no changes currently anticipated — the draft aligns\" (card 26). The agent did not edit it. The draft lets the Board, or the Audit Committee \"if delegated,\" approve waivers. The Charter gives the Audit Committee authority over executive-officer and director waivers (§V.D.2) and bars subcommittee delegation of that authority (§VI). The record does not show a Board delegation, so the resolution should be checked before the March 10 pre-meeting.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Memorandum outstanding items (15).<\/strong> These are: remove the Cambridge reception number from the Whistleblower Policy; engage a 24/7 hotline vendor ($85,000); complete the Brenner remedial review before rollout; Audit Committee review of the Sørensen consulting arrangement before proxy season; Audit Committee ratification of the Solstice contract, with Castellano and Chang recused; an initial related-party inventory and D&amp;O questionnaires; amendments to the Insider Trading Policy; a training program ($120,000); a standalone Data Privacy Policy by about April 2025; the CCO search ($275,000 to $325,000, Q2 2025); distribution of the Code and collection of acknowledgments from about 410 personnel; website posting and Exhibit 14 filing; the CEO letter; conforming amendments to the Whistleblower and Social Media policies and address corrections; and a Swiss-law supplement for the Basel office.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Controller not named.<\/strong> The sources do not name the controller. The Code covers that role by title, and the memorandum's request for a name is open.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Sørensen consulting arrangement.<\/strong> Henrik Sørensen, the CEO's husband, is a biostatistics consultant at $18,000 a month (about $216,000 a year), above the $120,000 Item 404 threshold (card 22; memorandum). The memorandum recommends Audit Committee review. The record contains no decision.<\/li>\n<li><strong>CFO share purchase.<\/strong> CFO Rachel Dominguez bought 5,000 shares on October 28, 2024, before Phase 2 results were announced on November 15, 2024. Calloway's notes say no pre-clearance was required at that time. The memorandum recommends retrospective treatment. The record contains no decision.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Solstice contract.<\/strong> A $1.2 million bioinformatics contract with Solstice Data Analytics, Inc., a Ridgepoint portfolio company, was approved by the Board in November 2024. Directors Castellano and Chang voted without recusal, and the Audit Committee was not consulted (card 22).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Hotline.<\/strong> The Code describes the 24/7 multilingual hotline as \"once established.\" No vendor is engaged and no number is given. The Whistleblower Policy lists business hours only, per the agent's notes (card 22).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>6.3 Dates<\/h3>\n<p>The scenario dates are early 2025: the email is dated January 27, the memorandum February 28, and adoption March 18, 2025. Today is October 22, 2026. The March 2025 milestones, the Q2 2025 CCO hire, and the April 2025 policy deadline have all passed, so every date in the drafts must be reset before use.<\/p>\n<\/section>\n\n<section id=\"verification\">\n<h2>7. How far the work was checked<\/h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Check<\/th><th>Cards<\/th><th>Result<\/th><\/tr><\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Zip and OOXML structure of both .docx files<\/td><td>31<\/td><td>Passed for both files. Each contains the core parts and a non-trivial document body.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Plain-text conversion of both files<\/td><td>33, 55, 64<\/td><td>Converted. The agent reports about 8,400 words (Code) and about 4,700 words (memo).<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Required terms present (occurrence counts)<\/td><td>34, 36, 38, 40, 42, 43, 56, 58<\/td><td>First pass returned zeros for several terms, including \"National Labor Relations\" (card 34). The agent reran on flattened text (card 36). Its \"Chang\" hits were false positives for \"changes\" (card 38).<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Private matters absent from the Code<\/td><td>37<\/td><td>The agent reported that Brenner, Solstice, Henrik Sørensen, Ridgepoint, and the CFO trade do not appear in the Code.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Section 12 title matches the draft resolution<\/td><td>59<\/td><td>Matches.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Hotline wording<\/td><td>61<\/td><td>Reviewed. The Code uses \"once established\" and omits the reception number.<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The checks did not establish: that any requirement is legally correct; that the citations match the rules; that the files open and display correctly in Word or another viewer; that the table of contents generated by the build is populated; or that every statement of fact matches its source. The agent made spot-checks against the source documents, covering the email's lack of a facilitation reference, the \"Section 5.3\" source, policy §4, the Charter and resolution wording, the Thornberry hotline details, and the memorandum's outstanding list.<\/p>\n<p>Most authorities in the drafts were not checked against any text in the record. They include Nasdaq Rule 5610, SOX 406 and Item 406, the Item 404 $120,000 threshold, the four-business-day Form 8-K Item 5.05 deadline, SOX 301 and Rule 10A-3, SOX 806, Dodd-Frank 21F, the FCPA facilitation exception, the UK Bribery Act 2010, Swiss Criminal Code art. 322ter, the revDSG, GDPR, HIPAA, 21 CFR Part 11, the PhRMA and EFPIA codes, Rule 10b5-1, and Section 16. The agent's fact inventory (card 22) shows these came from its own knowledge.<\/p>\n<\/section>\n\n<section id=\"next\">\n<h2>8. What the user should do next<\/h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Have qualified counsel review the Code and the memorandum, and check every regulatory citation against the primary text.<\/li>\n<li>Correct the \"Section 5.3\" attribution in the memorandum before it circulates, and in the fact inventory.<\/li>\n<li>Open both .docx files in Word or another viewer and check layout, the table of contents, and the appendix.<\/li>\n<li>Check the draft resolution's waiver language against the Charter, confirm whether a Board delegation to the Audit Committee is needed, and finalize the resolution.<\/li>\n<li>Name the controller and confirm the covered persons under Section 12.<\/li>\n<li>Assign owners and dates to the 15 outstanding items. Start with the hotline vendor and the Brenner review, which the memorandum says must precede rollout.<\/li>\n<li>Decide how the Audit Committee will handle the CFO share purchase, the Sørensen consulting arrangement, and the Solstice contract, as the memorandum recommends.<\/li>\n<li>Reset every date in the drafts to the current calendar before using them.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/section>\n\n<\/main>\n<\/body>\n<\/html>\n"}, "same_run": "harvey-labs--claude-code--corporate-governance-draft-compliance-regulatory"});
